\par\par \b \uc2  Japan Hot Stocks-Brokers higher, exporters rebound. \b0\par\par\uc2 712 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Juni 2002\par 06:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2      TOKYO, June 24 (Reuters) - The following stocks are on the \par \par \uc2   move on Monday: \par \par \uc2     **BROKERS HIGHER ON GROWING VIEWS RECENT MARKET FALL OVERDONE** \par \par \uc2  Daiwa Securities Group , Japan's second-biggest brokerage, up 22 yen or 3.01 percent at 754, while its bigger rival Nomura Holdings up 3.18 percent at 1,718. \par \par \uc2  Given that New York is hovering near the three-year lows hit in the weeks immediately after the September 11 attacks and Tokyo's TOPIX index is approaching the 17-year lows hit in February, some investors have started to look for bargains which has improved sentiment in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  The brokerage subindex jumped 2.99 percent, far outperforming a 0.33 percent rise in the TOPIX. 0427 GMT **TOYOTA REVERSE LOSSES AFTER \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2 ** \par \par \uc2  Toyota Motor Corp , \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's biggest automaker, up 0.98 percent at 3,080, reversing morning losses after the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in early afternoon to prop up the dollar against the yen. \par \par \uc2  Consumer electronics giant Sony Corp, which gets two-thirds of its revenues from overseas, up 90 yen or 1.5 percent at 6,110. \par \par \uc2  Earlier in the day, major high-tech and auto exporters extended recent losses, dampened by the dollar's rapid fall late last week as well as continuing falls in U.S. stocks and an uncertain outlook for consumer demand there. \par \par \uc2  A strong yen eats into Japanese exporters' overseas earnings when repatriated into yen. \par \par \uc2  Sony, the most actively traded issue by value on the first section of the Tokyo Stock Exchange, hit a four-month low of 5,910 soon after opening. 0400 GMT **YAMADA DENKI SOARS AFTER MORGAN STANLEY UPS TARGET PRICE** \par \par \uc2  Consumer electronics retailer Yamada Denki Co Ltd up 6.26 percent or 610 yen at 10,350 yen after Morgan Stanley raised the stock's target price to 13,000 yen from 12,000 on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The company's acquisition of unlisted bulk discount retailer Daikuma will increase customer-pulling power and boost earnings, Morgan Stanley analyst Taizo Demura said in a note to clients. Demura maintained an "overweight" rating on the stock. \par \par \uc2  Japan's largest retailing group, Ito-Yokado Co Ltd , said last month it sold its entire 88.4 percent stake in Daikuma to Yamada Denki for 16.8 billion yen ($138.1 million). 0200 GMT **JR EAST SOLID, BENEFITS AS TECHS AND EXPORTERS SHUNNED** \par \par \uc2  East Japan Railway Co up 1.09 percent or 6,000 yen at 556,000 yen, outperforming a 0.80 percent fall in the benchmark Nikkei index . \par \par \uc2  Last week the government sold its remaining stake of 500,000 shares in JR East at 532,000 yen per share. \par \par \uc2  Investors are looking to diversify their portfolios away from more volatile technology-based stocks and into stocks less vulnerable to external demand, traders say. \par \par \uc2  This move is expected to support JR's share given the shaky U.S. stock market and a stronger yen. 0130 GMT \par \par \uc2  **PAPER MAKERS UP, STRONGER YEN HELPS** \par \par \uc2  Oji Paper Co Ltd up 3.45 percent or 22 yen at 660 yen, helping to push the paper and pulp sector subindex up 2.93 percent. \par \par \uc2  Paper makers benefit from a stronger yen, which lowers their procurement costs from abroad. \par \par \uc2  Earlier this month Merrill Lynch raised its view of the Japanese paper industry to "slightly bullish" from "bearish" following the failure of mid-sized paper maker Nippon Kakoh Seishi Co Ltd . \par \par \uc2  Hokuetsu Paper Mills Ltd up 4.19 percent or 30 yen at 746 yen. 0057 GMT **MEGABANKS DOWN ON BAD-LOAN CONCERNS** \par \par \uc2  Mizuho Holdings Inc , world's largest bank by assets, down 3.44 percent or 9,000 yen at 253,000 yen. Sumitomo Mitsui Banking Corp down 3.78 percent or 22 yen at 560 yen. \par \par \uc2  Selling prompted by a report in the Nihon Keizai Shimbun on Monday that major banks may not be able to meet a deadline imposed by regulators to dispose of bad loans within three years because of objections from Japanese regional credit agencies. \par \par \uc2  Traders say further falls in Japanese stocks would increase losses on banks' massive shareholdings, eroding their capital base and reducing their capacity to write off bad loans. 0035 GMT ($1=121.65 yen). \par \par \uc2   .  . (C) Reuters Limited 2002. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020624dy6o000ma\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
