\par\par \b \uc2  Euro Trades Above $0.98 For First Time Since Feb 2000 \b0\par\par\uc2 448 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Juni 2002\par 10:50\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  LONDON -(Dow Jones)- The euro is trading above $0.9800 for the first time since February 2000, making parity with the dollar within a short while a distinct possibility. \par \par \uc2  The dollar has succumbed to heavy selling across the board Monday, despite a bout of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asian trading. \par \par \uc2  And the euro is one of the main beneficiaries, climbing as high as $0.9818. \par \par \uc2  "The euro has a fair wind behind it now," said Neil MacKinnon, head of research at ECU, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , debt, and hedge fund group in London. "A test of parity is on the cards." \par \par \uc2  MacKinnon pointed out that the dollar selling has been broad-based, and from a whole host of market participants. \par \par \uc2  "And there's nothing in the pipeline to suggest that will come to an end soon," he added. \par \par \uc2  At 0900 GMT, the euro was at $0.9807, up from $0.9719 at the London open and $0.9712 late Friday in New York. \par \par \uc2  The aggressive dollar sell-off against the euro Monday is also sending the greenback down to new lows against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , such as the Swiss franc. \par \par \uc2  Support levels are being broken almost at will, with short- and long-term investors bailing out of the dollar en masse. \par \par \uc2  "We're seeing big flows," said the head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales at a major European bank in London. "Everyone's selling...and there's a lot more to come." \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell below CHF1.5000 Monday for the first time since October 1999. \par \par \uc2  At 0905 GMT, the dollar was at CHF1.5017, down from CHF1.5107 at the London open Monday and CHF1.5117 late Friday in New York. \par \par \uc2  A short while ago, it dipped as low as CHF1.4975. \par \par \uc2  The pound was at $1.5063, up from $1.4995 earlier Monday and $1.4967 late Friday. \par \par \uc2  London-based dealers say the green light to bail out of dollars was the euro's reaction to BoJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the Asian session. \par \par \uc2  The BoJ acted upon \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  instructions to buy dollars for yen in order to stem the yen's appreciation. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen duly spiked up to Y122.80 from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels of around Y121.10, but the euro dipped only 30 pips. \par \par \uc2  Broader dollar selling quickly ensued, notably against the euro and Swiss franc. \par \par \uc2  "It's just a dollar weakness thing, not Swissie strength, or euro strength," a dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Indeed, the dollar is now perilously close to those \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels, trading at Y121.30 moments ago. \par \par \uc2  -By Jamie McGeever, Dow Jones Newswires; +44-20-7842-9483; jamie.mcgeever@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020624dy6o000ig\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
