\par\par \b \uc2  Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 : Dollar Soars After Japan MOF Sells Yen Again \b0\par\par\uc2  By Jonathan Soble \par \uc2 766 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Juni 2002\par 08:14\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- The dollar jumped nearly two yen in Asia Monday after Japanese authorities \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the 5th time in a month to boost the sagging U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but traders said Tokyo is fighting a losing battle. \par \par \uc2  After several early warnings it was getting ready to take action, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Finance Ministry struck. Finance Minister Masajuro confirmed the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the reason for it: \par \par \uc2  "The recent rapid movements of the dollar-yen exchange rate in the markets could have undesirable implications for the Japanese and the world economy," he said in a statement. \par \par \uc2  The yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  stopped an early dollar slide in its tracks at Y121.00 and sent the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  soaring to a high of Y122.79. \par \par \uc2  But in a sign of market participants' gloomy view of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's prospects, the dollar almost immediately turned South again, drifting back down below Y122.00 mark later in the day. \par \par \uc2  "There was some immediate impact from the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but it will be short-lived if (the MOF) doesn't keep selling yen," said Aozora Bank's chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager Shuji Takano. \par \par \uc2  Weak U.S. stocks are chiefly responsible for the stubborn dollar bearishness, traders said. Without a rebound in U.S. equities and renewed confidence in Washington's willingness to support its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Japan's ability to bolster the dollar and fend off a strong yen will be limited, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Eisuke Sakakibara, Japan's former vice finance minister for international affairs - commonly know as "Mr Yen" for the sway he held over the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  during his tenure, told Dow Jones Newswires Monday before the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  made its move that MOF wants to keep the dollar above Y120. \par \par \uc2  Bank of America \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Marshall Gittler said Japan can snuff out short-term speculative moves, but won't be successful in stopping the dollar's plunge - because it is broadbased and beyond Japan's control. Gittler forecasts the dollar will fall to Y115 by September. \par \par \uc2  "They can't (support the dollar/yen) indefinitely. This isn't a yen rally, it's a dollar collapse," Gittler said. \par \par \uc2  At 0515 GMT (1:15 a.m. EDT), the dollar was quoted at Y121.93, above Y121.12 late Friday in New York but still below its level of Y123.36 late Friday in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at $0.9700, slightly lower than $0.9708 late Friday in New York. The euro was at $0.9662 in Tokyo Friday. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, at 0515 GMT Monday, the euro was quoted at Y118.37, up from Y117.58 in New York late Friday. The euro was at Y119.19 in Tokyo late Friday. \par \par \uc2  (Corrected 0657GMT) \par \par \uc2  The finance ministry has fought surges in the yen with some success in the past, but with the MOF up against a broadly weak dollar this time around, it will likely get less and less bang for its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  buck. \par \par \uc2  "It's hard to keep justifying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Toru Umemoto, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Morgan Stanley in Tokyo. "It's not a just a strong yen problem." \par \par \uc2  Umemoto said the dollar will likely move between Y120-Y125 in the short- to medium-term. But he added that if the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to fall against other major units such as the euro, even further yen-selling by the MOF likely won't keep it above the Y120 mark. \par \par \uc2  Prior to today's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , some traders had speculated that the ministry would be unwilling to throw its weight around in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets ahead of this week's Group of Eight summit meeting in Canada. \par \par \uc2  But MOF officials seemed confident that their G8 counterparts - particularly in the United States - would not object to their active undercutting of the yen. \par \par \uc2  "The U.S. has to finance its current account deficit. Why not let the Bank of Japan do the work for them?" a Japanese city bank trader said. \par \par \uc2  Sterling was at $1.4977, up from $1.4948 in New York but lower than $1.5007 in Tokyo Friday. \par \par \uc2  -By Jonathan Soble, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; jonathan.soble@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  The chart of late Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates that accompanied Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been replaced with an enhanced \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  fixture that is updated hourly. \par \par \uc2  Subscribers can find this table: \par \par \uc2  -On Telerate pages 4046 and 1087. \par \par \uc2  -On Dow Jones Newswires by searching the code N/IFX \par \par \uc2  -On Reuters by entering FRX or INT \par \par \uc2  -On Bloomberg by entering NI FRX \par \par \uc2  1074 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020624dy6o000bf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
