\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  SURVEY: Euro Seen 99 Cents End-Sept; Parity Yr-End \b0\par\par\uc2  By John Parry and Tyler Lifton \par \uc2 1,567 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Juni 2002\par 23:38\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Having last traded above $1.000 almost two and a half years ago, the euro has the hitherto elusive goal of parity with the dollar within its grasp, but  won't actually reach that bar until the end of the year, according to a survey of the major \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  banks. \par \par \uc2  Although one \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst said the euro might even touch parity within a week, the median forecast of 15 out of  18 banks that responded to a Dow Jones Newswires survey Monday predicted the euro reaching $0.9900 by the end of September and being one-for-one with the dollar by year-end,  up from its $0.9704 level late Monday in New York. \par \par \uc2  Reaching that level would mark a major threshold crossed  for the euro and reflect a deep shift in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market perceptions, revolving largely around the dollar's ebbing fortunes. As sentiment surrounding U.S. assets has turned nastier during the past few weeks, exacerbated by accounting scandals and concerns over U.S. corporate profitability, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists have been marking down their forecasts for the dollar's near-term performance. \par \par \uc2  The greenback has slipped sharply against its two major rivals, losing 2.3% against the yen and 2.7% against the euro in the past week alone. This year so far, the dollar has slid 7.2% against the yen and 8.3% against the euro and so far this quarter is down over 10% against both of those key counterparts. \par \par \uc2  According to the banks surveyed, the yen is set to weaken partially from current levels, aided by the threat of  further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Although the dollar's slippage against the yen has been highlighted by five bouts of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  - via the BOJ, designed to weaken the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  during  the past five weeks, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders are most closely focused on the euro, which is perceived to be the chief beneficiary of the dollar's malaise. \par \par \uc2  That's a far cry from the outlook just three weeks ago. In a Dow Jones Newswires survey published June 3, only three  out of ten banks responding said they anticipated the euro reaching parity this year, with the median estimate then at $0.9800 by the end of the year. In fact the euro crashed briefly above $0.9800 cents on Monday, trading as high as $0.9813, its strongest level since Feb. 29, 2000. In that sense, forecasting the euro at $0.9900 by the end of September is hardly an adventurous wager. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Might Encounter Some Roadblocks\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But many analysts anticipate the euro might have difficulty in\par consolidating its existing gains, before once again pressing higher.\par Nonetheless, the fact that a broad sample of major banks anticipate the\par euro hitting parity this year is highly significant. And few \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign\par exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market specialists would have had this outlook back in the\par first quarter."Had you asked me three months ago, I would have\par definitely...guessed (the euro) would have stalled out at 93" cents,\par said Jane Foley, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist with Barclays Capital in London.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Although the fact that the euro has been advancing against the dollar\par hasn't been surprising, "the speed at which events have unfolded has,"\par said Shahab Jalinoos, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist with UBS Warburg in London.\par The pace of the move reflects "the dramatic downturn in sentiment\par regarding U.S. asset markets, combined with an apparent lack  of concern\par by U.S. officials regarding dollar weakness," Jalinoos said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Hints from Group of Seven leading industrial nations and  European\par Union officials that there will be more "tolerance of euro strength"\par than they previously indicated, "has lifted the roof on the euro's\par upside potential," said Tim Stewart, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist with\par Morgan Stanley in New York.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Many \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategists have cited the U.S. stock market's slide as\par a primary reason for marking down expectations for the dollar. The\par lackluster performance of U.S. equities has also added weight to the\par view that the dollar's broad decline "is more of a secular than a\par cyclical story," said Stewart.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Such factors, combined with more portfolio flows heading  Europe's way\par during the past two months have stirred a quiet conviction that the\par euro's latest advance is more than  another flash in the pan. This time,\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  traders sense, it might be for real.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Analysts have made similar pronouncements numerous times  in the\par single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's brief history and on every one of those occasions, the\par euro has disappointed, a trend that's burnished its reputation as the\par king of failed rallies. Yet during those previous attempts, the euro was\par swimming against the tide of a generally strong dollar, buoyed by\par foreign investors' voracious appetite for U.S. assets and their faith in\par the vitality and flexibility of the U.S. economy.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But now, the bearish perception of the dollar, also partly stemming\par from the risks posed by the record U.S. current account and trade\par deficits, is now much more pronounced, leading strategists to update\par their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  forecasts with more frequency than usual. The banks that\par have recently redrawn their forecasts include Citibank, which last week\par raised its end-September projection for the euro to $1.0200 from the\par previous $0.9800.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Some other banks, including JP Morgan which plans to release new\par forecasts on July 1, are currently reworking their projections.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Views Differ On Pace Of Dlr Decline\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Opinions differ about the speed of the next stage of the  euro's\par rally. The single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could gain steadily against the dollar,\par reaching parity by the end of August, said T.J. Marta, senior analyst at\par Citibank in New York.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Yet Stewart of Morgan Stanley said he wouldn't be surprised to see the\par euro hit parity within a week. What would sustain such a move would be\par the support of those so-called longer-term investors, including pension\par funds and  companies that have so far not markedly pared back their\par dollar-denominated assets.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Major investment funds and central banks "still haven't  lightened up\par on dollars significantly," giving the euro in  particular the\par opportunity to make further gains against the greenback, should these\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market participants start to reduce their dollar\par holdings, said David Mozina, director of fixed income and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign\par exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  research at Banc of America Securities in New York.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Despite the bearish medium-term dollar outlook, sentiment may not\par remain so poor through the end of this year, some expect, based on the\par anticipated growth differential between the U.S. economy and major\par rivals.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By the autumn, the U.S. economy is expected to be performing more\par strongly, helping the dollar stage at least a partial recovery, several\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategists said. And against the yen, aided by the threat of\par further \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the dollar is expected to remain fairly stable,\par firming to Y125 by the end of the third quarter and remaining there\par through the end of June 2003, according to the median estimates of\par strategists consulted.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Table Of Banks' \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Forecasts For Euro/Dollar\par Euro/Dlr On:        End Sept  End Dec    End June 2003\par Bank Of America    $0.9900   $1.0000      $1.0400\par Barclays           $1.0000   $0.9500      $0.8500\par BNP Paribas        $1.0200   $1.0200      $1.0500\par Brown Brothers     $0.9600   $1.0000      $1.0000\par Citibank           $1.0200   $0.9900      $0.9700\par CSFB               $1.0500   $1.0300      $1.0000\par Daiwa              $0.9900   $1.0000      $0.9500\par Deutsche Bank      $0.9800   $1.0000      $1.0500\par FleetBoston        N/A       N/A          N/A\par Goldman Sachs      $0.9300   