\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Options: Vols Little Changed Despite Dollar Dive \b0\par\par\uc2 459 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Juni 2002\par 20:59\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Volatilities implied by one-month euro/dollar and dollar/yen \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  options are little changed Monday, compared with levels earlier in London, as the dollar continues to fall against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  With the dollar under pressure, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , acting on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bought dollars for yen during Tokyo trade as it struggled to prevent the higher Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from halting a fragile recovery in the world's second-largest economy. \par \par \uc2  This is the fifth round of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the BOJ in as many weeks. \par \par \uc2  The dollar is also still under pressure from the euro, which surged above $0.9800 during London trading, hitting a session peak of around $0.9813, its strongest point since Feb. 29, 2000. \par \par \uc2  But the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has since slipped back a bit and analysts say the next level of resistance is around the $0.9820 area. \par \par \uc2  This strong performance by the dollars' counterparts has volatilities mostly on the rise when compared with levels last week, said Thomas Rogers, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at International Financing Review. \par \par \uc2  Rogers said one-month euro/dollar volatilities are "the highest since September 2001 as traders are finally starting to capitulate out of short volatility positions." \par \par \uc2  At around 2 p.m. EDT (1800 GMT) in New York, the euro was at $$0.9722, down from $0.9787 in London but up from $0.9712 late Friday in New York. The dollar was at Y121.54, up from Y121.10 in late New York dealings Friday. \par \par \uc2  Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was also at CHF1.5092, down from CHF1.5117 late Friday, and sterling was at $1.5009, up from $1.4967 late Friday. \par \par \uc2  In the over-the-counter market, one-month at-the-money dollar/yen options imply volatility of 10.30%-10.60%, flat from earlier in London. \par \par \uc2  One-month euro/dollar option volatilities are at 11.50%-11.90%, down just a touch from 11.60%-11.90% earlier in London. \par \par \uc2  Volatility measures the expected range in the value of an asset over the period of the contract. Volatilities on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  options gauge the anticipated moves in the underlying \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with a higher volatility implying a wider trading range. \par \par \uc2  One-month risk-reversals on dollar/yen favor yen calls over dollar puts at 1.00%-1.40%, again flat from earlier in London. \par \par \uc2  Risk-reversals measure the market preference for either put or call options by the premium or discount applied. \par \par \uc2  Euro calls are favored over the dollar at 0.90%-1.30%, also unchanged from earlier in London. \par \par \uc2  -By Kristyne E. Demske, Dow Jones Newswires; kristyne.demske@dowjones.com; 201-938-4535 \par \par \uc2  1077 4127 44427 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020020624dy6o0099d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
