\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  View: Dollar Slump Sparks Talk Of Euro Parity Soon \b0\par\par\uc2  By Grainne McCarthy \par \uc2 829 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Juni 2002\par 00:00\par \uc2  Dow Jones Emerging Markets Report \par \uc2 EM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Has the dam of dollar support finally burst? \par \par \uc2  Last week, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  suffered its biggest weekly drop in 10 months against the euro, at the same time clocking up its largest single-day decline against the yen since early March. Under pressure across the board, the dollar has slumped 10.3% against the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  so far this quarter, 10% compared to the yen and Swiss franc and 15% against the Norwegian krone. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is also under pressure against its so-called commodity bloc cousins, the Australian, New Zealand and Canadian dollars. In fact, apart from the bulk of Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar has effectively slumped across the board. \par \par \uc2  Even a slew of potentially supportive data this week - including U.S. May durable goods on Wednesday and final first quarter gross domestic product on Thursday - aren't expected to provide much relief for the bruised greenback. \par \par \uc2  Indeed, the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market's current pattern of reacting more strongly to negative U.S. data than to positive data now seems to have skewed even further, with the dollar sliding disproportionately on weak data, but also failing to rally even on positive news. The dollar isn't expected to garner any fresh support from the Federal Open Market Committee's meeting this week, with the Fed widely expected to leave key interest rates unchanged. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's demise has left analysts scrambling to revise their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  forecasts, with many now firmly convinced the euro could reach the elusive bar of parity with the dollar in the very near future. \par \par \uc2  "This is clearly the turn for the dollar," said Mike Rosenberg, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Deutsche Bank. "We could easily be at parity within the next weeks." \par \par \uc2  While many analysts believe the slide doesn't qualify as an absolute collapse - largely because the declines have been mostly of the steady and grinding rather than plummeting variety - the momentum of dollar losses certainly paves the way for a straight line decline of sorts in the greenback. \par \par \uc2  "We're at the point where it becomes self-reinforcing," said Lara Rhame, an economist at Brown Brothers Harriman in New York. "If people sell because it's overvalued, that's an issue, but if they sell because it's plunging, then it becomes self-fulfilling." \par \par \uc2  Investors already got a taste of this phenomenon last Friday, when the dollar sunk just under three yen in one day, after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokowa effectively ruled out \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a remarkable development given \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's long-standing support of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a monetary policy tool. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said Shiokawa's comments could have longer-term implications for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , particularly amid a sense that Japan's Group of Seven partners oppose Japan's efforts to deliberately manipulate its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "Saying `we have no power to dominate the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market' was about as foolish a comment you can get under the circumstances," said Alan Ruskin, research director at 4Cast financial consultancy in New York. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on four separate occasions over the past month, buying dollars for yen, in an attempt to slow the rapid appreciation of the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which it believes could undermine Japan's fragile cyclical recovery. \par \par \uc2  The strategy reaped some measure of success, but hasn't helped to turn yen sentiment around, particularly as the broad \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market has turned increasingly bearish on the dollar. Analysts say this dollar weakness might also be giving the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  cause for hesitation. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think the Japanese have any interest in stepping in front of the globe and turning against the dollar," said Rhame. \par \par \uc2  The dollar is being depressed by a host of factors, ranging from geopolitical tensions to concerns about accounting standards, corporate profits and the growing prospect - according to some economists - that the Federal Reserve might not raise interest rates at all this year. \par \par \uc2  This leaves investors even more intent on switching funds out of dollar-denominated assets in favor of destinations where they can earn higher returns, potentially leaving the U.S. with no choice but to jack up interest rates in order to attract enough capital to finance its burgeoning current account deficit. This, in turn, could stymie real economic activity, further reducing the appeal of the U.S. to foreign investors. \par \par \uc2  Even though growth prospects in the euro zone certainly don't look any better than this side of the Atlantic, the euro has been a key beneficiary of the dollar's woes, surprisingly managing to make solid progress almost on a daily basis, in the process shrugging off its reputation as the king of failed rallies. "The interesting thing is it's been extraordinarily orderly," said Ruskin. "But we're just accumulating solid gains, day in day out." \par \par \uc2  -By Grainne McCarthy; Dow Jones Newswires; 201 938 2381; grainne.mccarthy@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1141 4137 \par \par \uc2 Dokument em00000020020623dy6n000b5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
