\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar supported by fear of BOJ but still fragile. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Chikafumi Hodo \par \uc2 928 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Juni 2002\par 08:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 21 (Reuters) - The dollar briefly rose on Friday in a market worried about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but amid confusion about the Japanese stance it quickly fell back under the weight of a widening U.S. current account gap and weak U.S. stocks. \par \par \uc2  After volatile trade in the morning, the dollar stabilised in the afternoon after a brief blip up to 124.00 yen, as large dollar bids from a Japanese bank sparked speculation that Japanese authorities may have conducted dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The market calmed down a little after senior Finance Ministry official Zembei Mizoguchi said the ministry had not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Caution over \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was strong from early morning, but sporadic bids from pension funds, as well as verbal threats from two top Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  diplomats, provided support to the dollar, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  But it lost ground as dealers questioned \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's willingness to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  after Finance Minister Shiokawa said the ministry had no power to dominate \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, adding that it could only watch trends with caution. \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa's comments caused some confusion in the market as traders thought Japan was determined to keep the yen weak because it is one of the most effective ways to shore up the economy at times when Japan is unable to take many monetary or fiscal steps. \par \par \uc2  "While the market was eager to sell the dollar, (Shiokawa's) comments provided a good excuse to sell dollars, given its weak sentiment," said a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at a Japanese trust bank. \par \par \uc2  "We really can't tell just from Shiokawa's comments whether Japan will actually loosen its grip," he said. \par \par \uc2  The dealer said, however, that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be refraining from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell the yen against the dollar because the government may not think it necessary to step in as the yen is weakening against the euro. \par \par \uc2  Japan's Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Haruhiko Kuroda welcomed the rise in the euro, which reached a two-year high of $0.9668 in New York overnight. \par \par \uc2  "As I have been saying, I welcome the rise in the euro, which had been undervalued. But the situation is not the same for the yen and I don't see a need for the yen to strengthen," he said. \par \par \uc2  At 0556 GMT, the dollar was quoted at 123.37/38 yen against Thursday's late U.S. level of 123.26/36 yen. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at 119.08/25 yen after rising to a fresh five-month high of 119.39. \par \par \uc2  The single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was at $0.9658/63 against its late U.S. level of $0.9665/70. \par \par \uc2  FOCUS STILL ON US \par \par \uc2  Still, worries about the Japanese economy are taking a back seat for now to concerns about the U.S. economy, and the dollar is likely to stay under pressure, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. trade deficit soared to a record level in April, as did the U.S. current account deficit - the broadest measure of trade including investment flows as well as goods and services - in the January-March quarter. \par \par \uc2  The market is worried that the current account gap, which amounts to about four percent of U.S. gross domestic product (GDP), may not be sustainable. \par \par \uc2  In the end, such a deficit could be forcibly adjusted by the market, through a sharp depreciation of the dollar, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "Structurally, the U.S. needs foreign capital to finance its deficit. So a fund outflow from the U.S. is fatal," said Kota Kimura, assistant manager at Shinkin \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Foreign investors, attracted in droves by the booming U.S. economy of the late 1990s with its huge acceleration in worker productivity, are now becoming sceptical. \par \par \uc2  "In the past, all the investment went to the U.S. But now people are starting to think the U.S. economy is more or less the same as others," said Takashi Toyahara, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Nomura Securities. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. share markets still suffer from economic uncertainty and worries about security issues. \par \par \uc2  On Thursday, the Dow Jones industrial average fell 1.36 percent and the Nasdaq composite index 2.14 percent. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was also weighed down by the news on Thursday that a German government official said the United States had not shown any concern about the recent fall in the dollar against the euro in talks preparing for a G8 summit at the end of June. \par \par \uc2  The news reinforced market speculation that the U.S. administration tacitly approves of a weaker dollar ahead of congressional elections in November. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 0606 GMT. All data taken from Reuters \par \par \uc2  with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at 2130 \par \par \uc2  GMT. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                        Last     US Close  % Chg  YTD %   2001 Cls\par                                 20 Jun.\par                        -----------------------------------------\par  Euro/dlr              0.9653   0.9647   +0