\par\par \b \uc2  Japanese officials send mixed signals on  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  policy. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Yonggi Kang \par \uc2 432 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Juni 2002\par 06:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 21 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market dealers in Tokyo were scratching their heads with one hand and selling the dollar with the other on Friday as government officials issued a series comments on the yen's recent strength against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa, who has been saying authorities will act if rates moved too quickly, surprised with a comment that the ministry had no power to dominate the markets. \par \par \uc2  "We can only take a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy whereby the ministry always watched the trend in the market with caution," he said. "We have no power to dominate the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market." \par \par \uc2  Some dealers interpreted this to mean Japan thinks it needs cooperation from other central banks to have any real impact on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates. This was taken as a signal to buy the yen, which rose quickly to 123.30 to the dollar from 123.60. \par \par \uc2  But the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise was short-lived. \par \par \uc2  A big order from a Japanese bank pushed it down to 124 in short order, stirring talk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  - talk quickly quashed by senior Finance Ministry official Zembei Mizoguchi. \par \par \uc2  "The market has been nervous about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . That's why it reacted to hastily," said Kota Kimura, assistant manager at Shinkin \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The morning started with the now-familiar comment from Japan's top financial diplomat, Haruhiko Kuroda, who reiterated to reporters that there was no change in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. \par \par \uc2  Government officials have said publicly this means authorities will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates change too quickly. \par \par \uc2  But many in the markets believe it also means authorities will step in if the yen rises to a level where it looks like a threat to Japan's export-led recovery. \par \par \uc2  Many dealers believe a dollar/yen rate of below 124 is that level. The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  four times recently when the dollar fell below 124. The dollar stood at 123.35 at midday. \par \par \uc2  "The U.S. economy is still strong so the situation does not warrant continued strength in the yen," Mizoguchi said. \par \par \uc2  There was talk that the BOJ might even come into the market later on Friday when trading is likely to be especially thin as England and Brazil kick off their World Cup quarter final. \par \par \uc2  "Trade will be extremely thin during the World Cup game and there's a risk the dollar could fall sharply during that time," said a Japanese trust bank dealer, adding that this could prompt the authorities to act. \par \par \uc2  The match starts at 0630 GMT. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020621dy6l008yb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
