\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar/Falls -2: Slides By Almost Two Yen In One Session \b0\par\par\uc2 631 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Juni 2002\par 15:26\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Buoyed by a sense that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at these levels, the dollar slid Friday in New York to its lowest level against the yen since Nov. 15, 2001, and remains broadly weaker against many of its other counterparts. \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell below Y122 in early New York trade, extending a slide that began in Asia and continued in European trading hours. The selloff has pushed the dollar down by almost two yen in a single session and moves the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  well below the zone in which Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  - acting via the Bank of Japan - has previously stepped into the market to weaken the yen. \par \par \uc2  This time however, comments by Japanese policy makers - including Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa and Bank of  \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Masaru Hayami - helped convince the market  that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is off the table, for now. \par \par \uc2  In remarks that appeared to sow confusion among \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  investors, Shiokawa said it was "natural for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates to move up and down, and if we fret  over such moves we will lose sight of overall economic conditions." He added, notably, that "we can't control the markets. All we can do in terms of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy is say we are always alertly watching markets." \par \par \uc2  Hayami meanwhile, said the strength of the yen was simply a result of the well-off in the dollar, comments that appeared to brush off concerns about the effects of a  stronger Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Early in New York, the dollar was at Y122.14, just off  its intraday low of Y121.90, and sharply weaker than Y123.31 earlier in London Friday and late Thursday in New York. The dollar is down sharply from its session high of Y123.85. \par \par \uc2  The euro is also under pressure against the yen, trading at Y117.72, down from late New York's Y119.16. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't as large a threat now, said Tim Mazanec, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Investors Bank & Trust in Boston. "We've got some indications overnight from members of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  that they might not come in right away," he said. \par \par \uc2  "This is just five days after the G7 meeting,"  he said. "Maybe there was some talk there that they should not be \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  as much as they have been." \par \par \uc2  Indeed, some analysts believe Shiokawa's comments represent a shift in Japan's' policy, in the wake of the meeting of finance ministers from the Group of Seven leading industrial nations last weekend in Halifax, Nova Scotia. \par \par \uc2  Analysts at Fleet Global Markets said Shiokawa's remarks "suggest Japan's lesson from the G7 ... was that there is no support for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." This theoretically  clears the way for further yen strength, particularly in a climate where the dollar is, in any case, broadly weaker. \par \par \uc2  "If there isn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and a lot of it, in the  next 48 hours, it's quite likely that the yen will rise right through Y120," the Fleet analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the euro was weaker against the dollar Friday, correcting lower in a widely expected bout of profit-taking following its sharp rise Thursday to new two-year highs. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was changing hands at $0.9639, down from $0.9655 at the London open Friday, and $0.9667 late Thursday in New York. \par \par \uc2  Similar factors are also driving sterling lower, to $1.4959 a short while ago from $1.4998 at the London open Friday and a 17-month high of $1.5004 late Thursday. The dollar is also at CHF1.5255, from CHF1.5228 earlier and CHF1.5365 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  -By Grainne McCarthy, Dow Jones Newswires; 201 938 2381; grainne.mccarthy@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  4164 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020621dy6l000tw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
