\par\par \b \uc2  Dlr Tumbles Vs Yen, Euro; Sparks Talk Of Major Breakdown \b0\par\par\uc2  By Grainne McCarthy \par \uc2 941 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Juni 2002\par 16:56\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Still feeling the effects of a major increase in the U.S. current account deficit and languishing equities, the dollar has tumbled against the yen  Friday in New York and has touched its lowest point against  the euro in over 26 months, prompting talk of a major breakdown in the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "We're at the point where it becomes self-reinforcing," said Lara Rhame, an economist at Brown Brothers Harriman in New York. "If people sell because it's  overvalued that's an issue, but if they sell because it's plunging then it becomes self-fulfilling." \par \par \uc2  In a remarkably volatile market, the dollar has fallen by almost two and a half yen so far this session, its largest single-day decline since March 7 and its weakest level against the yen since Nov. 15, 2001. \par \par \uc2  The surge in the yen - the speed of which caught some market participants by surprise - comes in the wake of comments by finance minister Masajuro Shiokowa late Thursday  in Tokyo, indicating that the Japanese monetary authorities  won't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market for now. \par \par \uc2  "It's natural for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates to move up and  down, and if we fret over such moves we will lose sight of overall economic conditions," Shiokawa said, adding that "we can't control the markets." \par \par \uc2  His remarks were interpreted as a green light to push the dollar down further, with some analysts arguing Shiokawa  may have been signaling an effective change in Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. Until now, that policy appeared to have been focused on taking active steps to stop an overly strong yen from derailing a fragile recovery in the world's second largest economy. \par \par \uc2  "It looks like Japan may be reconsidering their weak yen policy," said Paul Podolsky, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at FleetBoston Financial. "If there isn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a lot of it, in the next 48 hours, it's quite likely that the yen will rise right through Y120." \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on four separate occasions over the  past month, buying dollars for yen, in an attempt to slow the rapid appreciation of the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The strategy reaped some measure of success, but hasn't helped to turn around yen sentiment, particularly as the broad \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market appears to have turned increasingly bearish on the dollar. Analysts say this broad dollar weakness might  also be giving the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  cause for hesitation. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think the Japanese have any interest in stepping in front of the globe and turning against the dollar," said Rhame. \par \par \uc2  Although the dollar has rebounded slightly against the euro, it remains under broad pressure. The dollar is changing hands at Y121.44, its session low against the yen, and sharply weaker from its high of Y123.85 from earlier in the global session and Y123.31 late Thursday in New York. \par \par \uc2  The euro was trading at $0.9685, just shy of its peak around $0.9700 - its strongest point since April 5, 2000 - and up from $0.9667 late Thursday in New York. \par \par \uc2  In a swift-moving market, the dollar fell below the important level of Y121, briefly hitting its weakest point since Nov. 12 last year. Analysts said Shiokawa's comments could have longer-term implications for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , particularly amid a sense that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Group of Seven partners oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a policy tool. \par \par \uc2  "Saying `we have no power to dominate the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market' was about as foolish a comment you can get under the  circumstances," said Alan Ruskin, research director at 4Cast financial consultancy in New# York. \par \par \uc2  The dollar is being depressed by a host of factors, ranging from geopolitical tensions to concerns about accounting standards, corporate profits and the growing prospect - according to some economists - that the Federal Reserve might not raise interest rates at all this year. \par \par \uc2  This leaves  investors even more intent on switching their funds of out of dollar-denominated assets in search of  destinations where they can earn higher returns. \par \par \uc2  The underlying problems for the greenback were also solidly highlighted on Thursday with reports that the trade and current account deficits had widened to record levels, focusing fears about the destabilizing effects the U.S. financing gap might have on the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Even though growth prospects in the eurozone certainly don't look any better than this side of the Atlantic, the euro has been a key beneficiary of the dollar's woes, surprisingly managing to make solid progress almost on a daily basis, in the process shrugging off its reputation as the king of failed rallies. \par \par \uc2  "The interesting thing is it's been extraordinarily orderly," said Ruskin. "But we're just accumulating solid gains, day-in, day-out." \par \par \uc2  The other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have also benefited from the dollar's decline. \par \par \uc2  "The thing is, you really have to take it all in its wider context," said Ruskin. "Yes, the yen took up the running but to my mind that was a terrible signal for the dollar. The major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are just passing the baton between each other." \par \par \uc2  The dollar has also fallen against many of the so-called  commodity bloc \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , particularly the Australian dollar, and against many \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asia. \par \par \uc2  In Latin America however, the dollar hasn't suffered at all and remains sharply stronger across the board. The dollar is trading around 18-month highs against the Mexican peso and close to record levels against the Brazilian real. \par \par \uc2  -Grainne McCarthy; Dow Jones Newswires; 201 938 2381; grainne.mccarthy@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020020621dy6l0058y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
