\par\par \b \uc2  Euro to rise above parity vs dollar - Sakakibara. \b0\par\par\uc2 481 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Juni 2002\par 12:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 19 (Reuters) - Former Japanese financial diplomat Eisuke Sakakibara said on Wednesday he expected the euro to strengthen towards, and then go above, parity with the dollar. \par \par \uc2  But he said the dollar was unlikely to fall significantly against the yen because Japanese authorities were determined to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if it looked like dollar/yen was heading towards 120. \par \par \uc2  He gave no timeframe for his euro forecast. \par \par \uc2  "The major issue in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market will be the weakening of the U.S. dollar," Sakakibara, now at Keio University in Tokyo, said in a speech at a Reuters client function. \par \par \uc2  The yen was quoted around 124.10 to the dollar in late Asian trading on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara, who had a reputation for aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  when he was at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said the dollar could suffer as the U.S. economy had not performed to expectations. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara, known as "Mr Yen", left the ministry in July 1999 but is widely believed by many in the financial markets to still have some sway over Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think it is unreasonable to expect the euro to come back to the level of early 1999, which is more than parity vis-a-vis the U.S. dollar," he said. \par \par \uc2  The euro hit 95.43 cents earlier on Wednesday, up from about 86 cents in early February, a rise of more than 10 percent. \par \par \uc2  The yen was unlikely to rise as much, Sakakibara said. \par \par \uc2  "From what I know, the Japanese authorities will continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if there are signs that the yen/dollar rate will go beyond 120," he said. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market several times in the past few weeks when the dollar fell below 124 yen. \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell from near 134 yen in early April to a six-month low of 122.82 yen in late May. \par \par \uc2  In addition to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Japanese economic fundamentals would hold back the yen, Sakakibara said. \par \par \uc2  "Most structural reform has been postponed. So after the summer the Japanese economy may see a resurgence of structural problems, (such as) bankruptcies of construction companies and real estate companies," he said. \par \par \uc2  "From that perspective, I would not expect the Japanese yen to strengthen significantly vis-a-vis the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  "I would expect the dollar to be in a range between 122 to 127 yen, at least for some time to come," he said. \par \par \uc2  A worse-than-expected performance by the U.S. economy could lead to a reversal of the large capital inflows that supported the dollar for five or six years until 2001, Sakakibara said. \par \par \uc2  "In 2001, net capital inflows into the United States reached $450 billion," he said. "If there are doubts about the sustainability of a robust U.S. recovery, it is quite likely the capital flows may reverse." \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020619dy6j00lx8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
