\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar drops sharply, unable to benefit from data. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Daniel Bases \par \uc2 970 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Juni 2002\par 20:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, June 18 (Reuters) - The dollar fell to a 17-month low against sterling and tested a similar milestone versus the euro on Tuesday, unable to gain from the biggest rise in U.S. home construction in seven years and low inflation in May. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the lock-step relationship between the dollar and equity markets became decoupled on Monday as equities rallied and the dollar remained weak, and that pattern continued on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar's not taking a cue from the equity market because that is flat," said John McCarthy, director of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at ING Barings Capital Markets. "There has been a strong surge in sterling's value, which had been a big laggard, and that is helping it drag the dollar lower across the board," he said. \par \par \uc2  Sterling traded at a fresh 17-month high of $1.4923, a gain of 0.85 percent on the day. \par \par \uc2  The euro was up at 95 cents , a gain of 0.61 percent compared with Monday's New York close, but below Friday's 17-month high of 95.23 cents. \par \par \uc2  The dollar also suffered against the so-called commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The Australian dollar advanced 1.13 percent versus the dollar to trade at 56.43 cents . \par \par \uc2  U.S. stock indexes briefly danced in positive territory before stepping back into the negative column, with the Dow Jones industrial average off 0.11 percent ; the Nasdaq Composite index down 0.54 percent; and the S&P 500 stock index losing 0.14 percent. \par \par \uc2  Investors are concerned U.S. stocks are overvalued given their earnings prospects and the constant drumbeat of accounting scandals. That has left the dollar in a lurch. \par \par \uc2  "I think the stock market and the dollar are overlooking good economic news in the United States," said Gary Thayer, chief economist at A.G. Edwards & Sons, Inc. in St. Louis. \par \par \uc2  Groundbreaking for new homes jumped 11.6 percent last month, reversing two straight months of declines, while inflation on the consumer level was unchanged during the same period, giving the Federal Reserve ample time to delay interest rate increases and allow the U.S. economy to gather strength. \par \par \uc2  "We may be in a period where stronger growth may hurt the dollar because people believe that will widen the current account deficit through higher imports, and the (U.S.) equity market is overvalued," said Michael Rosenberg, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Deutsche Bank in New York. \par \par \uc2  A deficit creates a natural drag on the dollar as U.S. importers exchange it to purchase goods from overseas. U.S. trade data is due on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Investors remained wary of selling the dollar against the yen too aggressively, fearing \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's export-crimping strength, as they have done four times in recent weeks. \par \par \uc2  The dollar rose as high as 124.84 yen before slipping back to 124.39 yen, still a small gain on the day. The euro traded at 118.13 yen , up 0.62 percent on the day. \par \par \uc2  In the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  futures market, September euros were trading in positive territory, but down from a two-session high of $0.9462. Resistance for the contract was at $0.9489, the contract high reached on Friday, one trader said. \par \par \uc2  June yen futures, meanwhile, remained negative and confined to Friday's range of $0.008030 to $0.008109. Support for the contract was at $0.008011, the 30-day moving average, one analyst said. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill and White House Economic Adviser Lawrence Lindsey both reiterated there has been no change to the long-standing strong dollar policy. Their remarks had no effect on the market. \par \par \uc2  O'Neill also said U.S. economic fundamentals remain strong, with some early signs that business investment is picking up. \par \par \uc2  SNB RAISES REPO \par \par \uc2  The Swiss National Bank raised its repo rate by one basis point to 1 percent on Tuesday, but the Swiss franc stayed in a narrow range versus the euro at 1.4764 francs, barely changed on the day. \par \par \uc2  However, the dollar's weak stance left it trading at 1.5545 Swiss francs, off 0.52 percent on the day. \par \par \uc2  The repo rate had been at 0.99 percent since May 30 after the SNB started nudging rates gradually lower in late March to counter the impact of the strong Swiss franc. \par \par \uc2  SNB Vice Chairman Bruno Gehrig said the bank welcomed the euro's recent gains against the Swiss franc, adding that the franc's exchange rate posed the biggest risk to an economic recovery. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 1755GMT.  All data taken from Reuters \par \par \uc2  calculated from the levels at 4:30 p.m. (2030 GMT) in the \par \par \uc2  previous New York session. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                      Last     US Close   %     YTD %   2001\par                               17 Jun.  Change  Change  Close\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -------------------------------------------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Euro/dlr             0.9507   0.9442   +0.69   +7.62   0.8834\par Dlr/yen              124.34   124.28   +0.05   -5.14   131.08\par Euro/yen             118.21   117.40   +0.69   +2.12   115.76\par Dlr/swiss            1.5519   1.5626   -0.68   -7.37   1.6753\par Stg/dlr              1.4921   1.4