\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dlr gives way to euro before Wall St, not to yen. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Natsuko Waki \par \uc2 617 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Juni 2002\par 14:06\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 18 (Reuters) - The dollar stepped back against European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday, coming close to a recent 17-month trough on the euro as investors doubted whether Wall Street could sustain the previous session's bounce. \par \par \uc2  But the greenback managed to chalk up some gains against the yen as the market remained nervous about possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  U.S. stocks, which have been driving the dollar in recent weeks, jumped on Monday with the tech-driven Nasdaq rallying more than three percent. But U.S. stock futures were pointing to a lower opening on the day. \par \par \uc2  "Overall, the euro's rise this morning was reflecting some concerns that yesterday's phenomenon on Wall Street could be a temporary correction. U.S. asset markets are still weak and that causes concerns for non-dollar based investors," said Stacey Seltzer, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Brown Brothers Harriman. \par \par \uc2  Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official Zembei Mizoguchi said earlier he would continue to monitor \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates closely. Japan, concerned that a strengthening yen would damage its tentative export-led recovery, has sold yen on at least four days in recent weeks. \par \par \uc2  "Japan reminded everybody that they are still around and that helped the dollar against the yen," Seltzer said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said Japanese trust banks and pension funds had been big buyers of euros for yen, pushing the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to 118.27 yen by 1150 GMT, within a yen of its five-month high set last week. \par \par \uc2  This dragged the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  up to $0.9485, within half a cent of last week's 17-month peak. The dollar rose to 124.84 from 124.28 in late New York on Monday. \par \par \uc2  SNB RAISES REPO \par \par \uc2  The Swiss franc hugged narrow ranges versus the euro and the dollar on Tuesday after the Swiss National Bank raised its repo rate by one basis point to one percent. \par \par \uc2  The repo rate had been at 0.99 percent since May 30 after the SNB started nudging rates gradually lower in late March to counter the impact of the strong Swiss franc. \par \par \uc2  SNB Vice Chairman Bruno Gehrig said the bank welcomed the euro's recent gains against the Swiss franc, adding the bank has no bias towards raising or cutting rates. \par \par \uc2  Gehrig added the bank is keeping a close eye on the fragile Swiss economy and will act quickly if franc strength or weak growth elsewhere hurt a tentative recovery. \par \par \uc2  He stressed the franc's exchange rate posed the biggest risk to an economic recovery. The SNB left its benchmark rate target unchanged at its quarterly policy review last week. \par \par \uc2  "Now euro/Swiss is back around 1.4750, they probably feel confident of pushing rates up," said Rob Hayward, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN Amro. \par \par \uc2  The Swiss franc paid little reaction, trading at 1.4762, barely changed from late New York levels on Monday. Against the dollar it stood at 1.5570, compared with 1.5626 in late New York on Monday. \par \par \uc2  LOOKING AHEAD \par \par \uc2  Later in the day, U.S. May consumer price data and May housing starts will be released. But the main focus is on U.S. trade data for April and current account figures for the first quarter, both due on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "We are still seeing flows into the U.S. but it is not sufficient to finance its current account deficit," said Trevor Dinmore, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Deutsche Bank. \par \par \uc2  Both of Thursday's sets of data are expected to show widening deficits. \par \par \uc2  Traders also looked towards the announcement of the Bank of Japan's corporate sentiment "tankan" survey on July 1. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020618dy6i00o3e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
