\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Yen slips against euro and dollar, eye on Wall St. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sumeet Desai \par \uc2 663 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Juni 2002\par 10:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 18 (Reuters) - The yen fell against the dollar and the euro on Tuesday as investors remained nervous about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But the dollar could not match the euro's gains against the yen and slipped back against the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as dealers looked ahead to see whether Wall Street could sustain Monday's rally. \par \par \uc2  Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official Zembei Mizoguchi said on Tuesday he would continue to monitor \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates closely. Japan, concerned about its exporters' competitiveness, has sold yen on at least four days in recent weeks. \par \par \uc2  "We're seeing a bout of yen weakness. The Japanese authorities are maintaining their stance against yen strength," said Rob Hayward, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN-Amro. \par \par \uc2  "It's also probably a reaction to Japanese equities which have underperformed U.S. stocks." \par \par \uc2  Dealers said trust banks had been big buyers of euros for yen, possibly on behalf of Japanese pension funds, pushing the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to around 118 yen by 0744 GMT, up around half a percent on the day. \par \par \uc2  This also pushed the dollar up to 124.55 yen but the greenback fell a quarter percent against the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to around $0.9468, just over half a cent from 17-month lows hit last week. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said many players were anxious to build fresh euro-long positions, having seen the dollar struggle despite a rally on Wall Street. \par \par \uc2  "After seeing the dollar struggle despite rallies in U.S. stocks overnight, the market appears to be still looking for incentives to sell dollars," said Shogo Nagaya, a manager at Nomura Trust and Banking's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  section in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  SNB RAISES REPO \par \par \uc2  The Swiss franc was steady versus the euro and the dollar on Tuesday after the Swiss National Bank raised its repo rate by one basis point to one percent. The repo rate had been at 0.99 percent since May 30 after the SNB started nudging rates gradually lower in late March to counter the impact of the strong Swiss franc. \par \par \uc2  "Now euro/Swiss is back around 1.4750, they probably feel confident of pushing rates up," said Rob Hayward, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN Amro. \par \par \uc2  The Swiss franc stood at 1.4757, barely changed from late New York levels on Monday. Against the dollar it stood at 1.5584, compared with 1.5626 in late New York on Monday. \par \par \uc2  LINGERING WEAKNESS OF DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  After a plunge on Friday, U.S. share prices came back strongly on Monday with the Nasdaq Composite ending up over three percent while the Dow Jones Industrial Average rose 2.25 percent. \par \par \uc2  But dealers were not convinced that either U.S. stocks or the U.S. economy would continue to recover so strongly. \par \par \uc2  Nomura Trust's Nagaya said the dollar could fall towards $0.96-$0.9650 per euro in the medium term, but that the greenback's declines were expected to be modest against the yen given fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese authorities. \par \par \uc2  "Our stance is unchanged, it is immovable," Mizoguchi, head of the ministry's international bureau, told reporters. \par \par \uc2  Later in the day, U.S. May consumer price data and May housing starts will be released. But the main focus is on U.S. trade data for April and current account figures for the first quarter, both due on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "It (the current account figure) could further eat into the weak dollar sentiment," said Motoshi Imura, senior manager of the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  and treasury division at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  Both of Thursday's sets of data are expected to show widening deficits. \par \par \uc2  Traders also looked towards the announcement of the Bank of Japan's corporate sentiment "tankan" survey on July 1. \par \par \uc2  Though the report is expected to show an improvement compared with the previous survey, some traders said it would not automatically trigger further yen-buying against the dollar. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020618dy6i00gim\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
