\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Falls Against Yen, Euro Despite Strong Economic Data \b0\par\par\uc2  By John Hardy \par \uc2 1,077 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Juni 2002\par 00:18\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -- In another sign of the extent to which bearish sentiment is weighing on the dollar, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  virtually ignored resoundingly strong U.S. housing data Tuesday, slipping lower across the board and falling to fresh 17-month lows against the pound. \par \par \uc2  The euro also took advantage of the dollar's weakness, significantly ending the New York day above the important 95-cent level for the first time since Jan. 12, 2001, a move that potentially sets the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  up for a sustained move higher. \par \par \uc2  In late New York trade, the euro was at 95.12 U.S. cents, up from 94.78 cents in London and above 94.45 cents late Monday in New York. The dollar was at 124.29 yen, down from 124.65 yen in London and a touch below 124.38 late Monday in New York. The dollar was also at 1.5511 Swiss francs, down from 1.5633 francs. Sterling was at $1.4905 up from $1.4796. \par \par \uc2  Underpinned by the constant threat of renewed Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar initially made modest progress against the yen, touching an intraday high at 124.83 yen, but as the market's mood reverted to pessimism toward the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar drifted down to lows around 124.27 yen. "There's still a legitimate threat of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  -if the dollar falls back toward 123 yen," said Robert Lynch, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNP Paribas in New York. \par \par \uc2  In an all-too familiar trend, the greenback barely budged after the Commerce Department said May housing starts leaped 11.6%, wildly exceeding the 2.9% gain expected. The consumer price index for May was also better-than-expected, coming in unchanged from April, an improvement on the expected rise of 0.1%. CPI ex-food and energy rose 0.2%, in line with market forecasts. Retail sales data for the latest reporting weeks were also positive, with the BTM-UBS Warburg chain store sales index up 0.7%, and the Redbook retail sales monthly index up 2.1%. \par \par \uc2  But while the dollar got a brief lift from the data, it quickly fell back, proceeding to slip during the rest of the New York session, with weak stocks -- in the aftermath of Monday's 2.3% rally -- unable to render any assistance, once again convincing analysts that the dollar has embarked on a major downward track. \par \par \uc2  "There's no question the dollar has peaked," said Sung Won Sohn, vice president and chief economist at Wells Fargo Bank in Minneapolis. "The question is will it depreciate rapidly or slowly." \par \par \uc2  Dealers also said growing Mideast tension has kept pressure on the U.S. unit. \par \par \uc2  The dollar wasn't even to find much assistance from reiterations by both Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill and chief presidential economic advisor Lawrence Lindsey that the U.S. strong dollar policy remains unchanged. \par \par \uc2  "This is a no-win subject," Mr. O'Neill said during an interview with cable television network CNBC. "The only thing that I can say about the dollar that doesn't give somebody comfort in trying to make something new out of it is: same policy, no intent to change, strong dollar." \par \par \uc2  Sterling was very much the focus of the market Tuesday, with the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  soaring to a session high near $1.4929, its strongest point since Jan. 12, 2001. The pound also gained against its other major counterparts, underpinned by better-than-expected inflation data for May and a generally bullish mood. \par \par \uc2  The U.K. retail price index excluding mortgage interest payments rose 0.3% on the month in May and 1.8% on the year, posting the biggest decline since October 1993 and confounding experts, who had expected an 0.5% rise on the month and a 2.0% rise on the year. \par \par \uc2  Sterling sentiment has been improving ever since Rupert Murdoch said last week that his U.K. newspapers will oppose any government pro-euro campaign ahead of a referendum on the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The euro meanwhile, also looked well placed to test new highs against the dollar. Having flirted with the important psychological level of 95 cents throughout much of the New York trading day, the fact that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  managed to end the session above that threshold could set the euro's sights on important resistance around 95.73 cents. \par \par \uc2  Even those who anticipate the need for a short-term retreat for the euro, don't doubt its longer-term upward trend. "I would expect some retracement to around the 93 cent-area, but if that happens, dollar-bulls will have used all their powder and the 95-cent-barrier will be ready to be taken," said Jan Poser, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Sarasin Bank in Zurich. \par \par \uc2  In other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Brazilian real strengthened fleetingly, after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market during the afternoon, buying real for dollars in a bid to shore up the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The combined value of the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  was estimated by traders at about $50 million. \par \par \uc2  As a result of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's actions, the dollar fell back briefly to 2.690 Brazilian reals, before climbing again to about 2.714 reals in later trading, above the 2.706 real level around the time of Tuesday's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action. The move marks the second round of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in just a few days, part of a broad effort by Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to bolster the real, which fell last week to its second lowest level ever. But the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  will probably just slow down the decline of the real, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "It's really only stemming the tide...of divestments out of Brazil," and the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are "only holding back the inevitable over the short term," said Thomas Molloy, a trader at Bank Leumi in New York. \par \par \uc2  As well as falling hostage to concerns over Brazil's debt levels, the real has been hurt by a strong showing by Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva, of the left-leaning Workers' Party, in opinion polls ahead of the presidential elections in October. The markets' favorite candidate, former health minister Jose Serra, is lagging in the polls by nearly 20 points. \par \par \uc2  Write to John Hardy, Dow Jones Newswires at john.hardy@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 2002 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020618dy6i00795\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
