\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar fails to hold gains, yen hampered by BOJ. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Hideyuki Sano \par \uc2 887 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Juni 2002\par 09:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 17 (Reuters) - The dollar lost its early gains in Asia on Monday as worries about the U.S. stock market and a growing U.S. current account deficit chipped away at confidence. \par \par \uc2  But dollar-selling against the yen was discouraged by another fall in Japanese share prices and by expectations that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  below 124 yen to stop the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from appreciating too much against the greenback. \par \par \uc2  The weekend meeting of G7 finance ministers and central bankers in Canada had little bearing on trade. \par \par \uc2  The meeting's communique expressed confidence about the recovering world economy but made no mention of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The dollar was locked in a tight range through the day. \par \par \uc2  As of 0535 GMT, it stood at 124.21/26 yen, little changed from 124.14/24 in late trading in New York. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar's rise versus the yen last week was just an adjustment to its recent bearish trend. Now the adjustment seems to be over," said Toshiyuki Aoyagi, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Nissho Iwai Financial Link, part of trading house Nissho Iwai Corp. \par \par \uc2  The dollar has fallen from above 133 yen in April to below 123 last month, hurt by worries about the U.S. stock market and the widening U.S. current account deficit. \par \par \uc2  The dollar could come under further downward pressure after Thursday's release of U.S. first-quarter current account data, which are expected to show an increase in the deficit to $106.07 billion from a fourth-quarter deficit of $98.84 billion. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's recent fall against the yen came to a halt only after Japanese authorities, concerned a stronger yen could choke off Japan's export-led economic recovery, stepped into the market last month to buy dollars, pushing up the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Few dealers were willing to sell the dollar below 124 yen where the BOJ repeatedly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The market is unwilling to build new dollar-short positions now after seeing aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the dollar fell below 124 yen," a senior dealer at a Japanese bank said. \par \par \uc2  The yen was also weighed by weak Japanese share prices. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo's benchmark Nikkei share average closed down 2.35 percent on Monday. \par \par \uc2  "Although share prices are falling in other markets, the fall in the Nikkei average should be an excuse to limit dollar-selling because the economic recovery in Japan is still not convincing compared with others," said the Japanese bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  "If the Nikkei falls below 10,000 it would become more difficult to buy the yen," said Aoyagi of Nissho Iwai Financial Link. \par \par \uc2  SCANT INTEREST IN G7 \par \par \uc2  The market showed little reaction to the G7 meeting. \par \par \uc2  In Halifax, Canada, finance ministers of the G7 nations proclaimed optimism over the global economic recovery, but their communique made no mention of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  or share prices. \par \par \uc2  The communique said: "Since we last met (in April), growth in our economies has strengthened and should continue to consolidate throughout the year. \par \par \uc2  "We are thus confident about our future prospects." \par \par \uc2  Some dealers took the apparent nonchalance to be tantamount to approval of a weak dollar. \par \par \uc2  "The G7 didn't say anything about the recent fall of the dollar. That may suggest a little more fall of the dollar is okay for them," said another Japanese bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  On the other hand, U.S. Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill repeated his usual strong-dollar statement. \par \par \uc2  O'Neill initially declined to comment on \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues when asked about \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the yen's advance. \par \par \uc2  "Yes, we have a strong-dollar policy. There is no change of dollar policy. We don't have an intention of changing the dollar policy - because if I don't say it, you will interpret something different," O'Neill said. \par \par \uc2  The euro was also little changed at 94.50/60 cents and 117.43/60 yen, compared with 94.57/62 and 117.24/42 at the end of last week. \par \par \uc2  The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  failed to maintain a 17-month high of 95.25 reached in U.S. trade on Friday. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 0602 GMT. All data taken from Reuters with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at 2130 GMT. \par \par \uc2   Last     US Close  % Chg  YTD %   2001 Cls \par \par \uc2   14 Jun. \par \par \uc2   ---------------------------------------- \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Euro/dlr       0.9452   0.9456   -0.04    +6.15   0.8904\par  Dlr/yen        124.25   124.08   +0.14    -5.61   131.63\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Euro/yen    117.41   117.40   +0.01    +0.15   117.24\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Dlr/swiss      1.5622   1.5597   +0.16    -5.86   1.6594\par  Stg/dlr        1.4761   1.4767   -0.04    +1.51   1.4541\par  Can/dlr        1.5477   1.5465   +0.08    -2.76   1.5917\par  Aus/dlr        0.5598   0.5601   -0.05    +9.64   0.5106\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Euro/swiss  1.4769   1.4752   +0.12    -0.