\par\par \b \uc2  Japan MoF Kuroda: Will Be Active In Mkt As Needed \b0\par\par\uc2 456 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Juni 2002\par 19:20\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  LONDON -(Dow Jones)- The Japanese finance ministry will continue to monitor developments in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and will remain active as necessary, a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 ministry of finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said Monday. \par \par \uc2  "We continue to monitor market developments, and as necessary, we remain active in the market," said Vice Minister for International Affairs Haruhiko Kuroda. \par \par \uc2  Speaking at the Royal Institute of International Affairs, Kuroda said that the ministry will respond to erratic movements in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market that are not justified by economic fundamentals. The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  four times in as many weeks to hold the yen's appreciation against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "In the last several weeks we have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to stop excessive appreciation of the yen, which was not warranted from the viewpoint of fundamentals," Kuroda said. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate should move slowly if it is to continue to reflect fundamentals." \par \par \uc2  At 1730 GMT (1:30 p.m. EDT), the dollar was trading at Y124.36, up from Y124.14 both earlier in London and late Friday in New York. The euro was at Y117.42, up from Y117.16 earlier in London and little changed from Y117.44 in New York Friday. \par \par \uc2  -By Paul Hannon, Dow Jones Newswires; 44 207 842 9491; paul.hannon@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  (This item originally ran around 1753 GMT Monday) \par \par \uc2  LONDON (Dow Jones)--The Japanese finance ministry will continue to monitor developments in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and will remain active as necessary, a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 ministry of finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said Monday. \par \par \uc2  "We continue to monitor market developments, and as necessary, we remain active in the market," said Vice Minister for International Affairs Haruhiko Kuroda. \par \par \uc2  Speaking at the Royal Institute of International Affairs, Kuroda said that the ministry will respond to erratic movements in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market that are not justified by economic fundamentals. The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  four times in as many weeks to hold the yen's appreciation against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "In the last several weeks we have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to stop excessive appreciation of the yen, which was not warranted from the viewpoint of fundamentals," Kuroda said. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate should move slowly if it is to continue to reflect fundamentals." \par \par \uc2  At 1730 GMT (1:30 p.m. EDT), the dollar was trading at Y124.36, up from Y124.14 both earlier in London and late Friday in New York. The euro was at Y117.42, up from Y117.16 earlier in London and little changed from Y117.44 in New York Friday. \par \par \uc2  -By Paul Hannon, Dow Jones Newswires; 44 207 842 9491; paul.hannon@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020617dy6h00128\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
