\par\par \b \uc2  Further dollar selling seen post-G7 meeting. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Javier David \par \uc2 791 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Juni 2002\par 21:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, June 16 (Reuters) - The Group of Seven's collective silence on the dollar's weakening trend is likely to lead to further selling of the greenback in exchange markets - thereby vexing Japan's painstaking efforts to keep the yen weak, analysts said on Sunday. \par \par \uc2  In Halifax, finance ministers of the world's seven leading industrialized nations proclaimed optimism over the global economic recovery. G7 delegates in their joint communique made no mention of the turmoil currently engulfing financial markets, but several officials acknowledged to reporters that the sharp stock market decline was worrisome. \par \par \uc2  Some policymakers expressed concern about the sharp fall in U.S. stock prices and the secondary effect it had on their respective \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Though Europe has welcomed the euro's rally to a 17-month high vs. the dollar, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has discouraged traders from bidding the yen up by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken it. \par \par \uc2  Most vocal of the group was \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's finance minister, Masajuro Shiokawa, who said that "the softening stock market in the United States is a source of concern," adding that while G7 governments were mindful of recent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market gyrations, they had no plans to influence the trend. \par \par \uc2  "The slide in the dollar is an obvious issue, not only for the (downward) movement that has taken place since April but the risk that a much more severe slide could develop," said Andrew Delano, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at IDEAGlobal, a research firm in New York. \par \par \uc2  Japan, the world's second-largest economy, is emerging from a recession and requires a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stimulate global demand for its exports. \par \par \uc2  However, the dollar's across-the-board retrenchment has undercut Japan's ability to engineer a weaker yen. Japanese monetary authorities between late May and early June \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in global markets to sell the yen against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "Shiokawa in particular is probably the one to have the most alarm about where we go from here," Delano said. "Clearly Japan is not in a position where it can afford to see stimulus slip away, and one stimulus has been the (yen's exchange rate)." \par \par \uc2  At a news conference, U.S. Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill reiterated - albeit reluctantly - the long-standing "strong dollar" policy. He added that even though corporate malfeasance and fears of more terror attacks have shaken investors, the U.S.' economy is on steady ground and should not hamper a global recovery. \par \par \uc2  For years, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has relied upon the influx of capital to offset America's yawning trade imbalance, which customarily leads to a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The bludgeoning of major stock indexes to lows unseen since late September has been the prime contributor to dollar weakness. With share prices seen falling further, many analysts think the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's weakness will continue. \par \par \uc2  STOPPING A RUNAWAY TRAIN \par \par \uc2  Analysts say the G7's failure to openly rebuke Japan for its manipulation of the yen's exchange rate - which undercuts an oft-enunciated G7 tenet that exchange rates are best determined by the market - is likely to keep the market on guard for further yen-selling forays. \par \par \uc2  "By not mentioning \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , (the G7) has pretty much given Japan the green light to continue entering the market," said Lara Rhame, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Brown Brothers Harriman in New York. \par \par \uc2  Rhame added that because the greenback is under pressure against all key \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Japanese "will follow the yen against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the Asian regionals (baht, yuan, won, etc.) and the euro," because trying to stand in the way of broad dollar weakness would be a losing gamble. \par \par \uc2  In Europe's case, a stronger euro is seen helping contain inflation pressures, thus forestalling an inevitable rate hike by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  German Finance Minister Hans Eichel said that "economically speaking, a combination of a strong euro and low interest rates is better than the other way round." \par \par \uc2  Market observers interpret the United States' reticence about the greenback's downturn as a tacit approval of a weaker dollar. But analysts warn of the growing risk of a market overshoot, which would negatively impact all countries. Though the dollar's recent setback has been orderly, it could become problematic if the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell too far, too fast. \par \par \uc2  "On a broad trade-weighted basis the dollar has only fallen 2 percent so far this year," Brown Brothers' Rhame said. "But anything faster than that, such as half a percent or 1 percent a month, would cause concern." \par \par \uc2  Indeed, French Finance Minister Francis Mer told reporters that "if the euro continues to appreciate against the dollar, at some stage it might start to create some problems. But we're not there yet." \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020616dy6g00suz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\p