\par\par \b \uc2  O'Neill - US productivity likely boost profits-Japan MOF. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Akiko Mori \par \uc2 430 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Juni 2002\par 03:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HALIFAX, Nova Scotia, June 14 (Reuters) - U.S. Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill told his Japanese counterpart on Friday the U.S. economy has improved tremendously since last autumn, with corporate profits likely to get a boost in the coming quarters, a Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) official said. \par \par \uc2  "The U.S. (Treasury Secretary O'Neill) said that despite the lingering negative impact of Sept. 11, productivity in the United States is very high," and that this should help corporate profits, the official told reporters after a meeting between O'Neill and Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa. \par \par \uc2  The U.S.-Japan talks were held on the sidelines of the meeting of finance ministers from the Group of Seven industrialized nations in Halifax, Canada. \par \par \uc2  At his meeting with O'Neill, Shiokawa also painted a bright picture of the Japanese economy. \par \par \uc2  The two financial chiefs, however, refrained from discussing Japan's monetary policy or recent rapid movements in the dollar, the MOF official said. \par \par \uc2  G7 officials have made clear the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market would not be a major topic at the meeting, which is helping to prepare the ground for a summit of G7 leaders, plus Russia, in the Canadian Rocky Mountain resort of Kananaskis later this month. \par \par \uc2  But the dollar's fate has been in the spotlight, with \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  repeatedly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent weeks to stem the yen's rise against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a move that some U.S. lawmakers have criticized. \par \par \uc2  Financial markets are closely eyeing the degree of strength in the U.S. economic recovery and that of Japan. In the first quarter of 2002 Japan's economy, which has been mired in recession, logged its best growth rate in two years at an annualized rate of 5.7 percent, outpacing that of the United States in the same period. \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa outlined Japan's new set of policy measures at his meeting with O'Neill, and the latter expressed high hopes for the steps, the official said. \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa detailed five major policy steps - a plan to help cutting-edge industries, maintain fiscal prudence, introduce tax and deregulation reforms and clean up nonperforming loans. \par \par \uc2  "We are showing progress in the disposal of bad loans," the official quoted Shiokawa as telling O'Neill. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese banking industry's mountain of bad debt has been blamed for the nation's decade-long economic malaise, and the government has pledged to get rid of "core" bad debt - loans to borrowers at risk of bankruptcy or already bankrupt - within three years. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020615dy6f001p5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
