\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar down after blast at U.S. consulate. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Kazunori Takada \par \uc2 940 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Juni 2002\par 09:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 14 (Reuters) - The dollar slipped in afternoon Tokyo trade on Friday after a bomb exploded outside a U.S. consulate in Pakistan, raising fears of further attacks targetted at the United States. \par \par \uc2  "When incidents like this happen, we sell dollars since we are reminded of the terror attack on New York," a dealer at a major Japanese bank said. "But the move was exaggerated due to thin trade during the day." \par \par \uc2  Pakistani police said at least five Pakistanis were killed in a suspected suicide bomb attack outside the consulate in Karachi, which also destroyed a boundary wall and several cars. \par \par \uc2  During the day, the dollar remained bearish after weak U.S. retail sales figures and another fall on Wall Street, while the euro was unable to regain its bullish tone after failing to hold recent highs. \par \par \uc2  "Basically, heavy euro-selling against the yen overnight put overall downward pressure on the euro. The market appeared to be clinging on to that mood in Tokyo," said Koichi Abe, manager at Aozora Bank's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading section. \par \par \uc2  The euro has failed to sustain recent highs above $0.95 and 119 yen. \par \par \uc2  As of 0549 GMT, the dollar was at 124.62 yen, weaker than late New York's 124.86. \par \par \uc2  The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was steady at 94.50 cents compared to 94.27 in late U.S. trade. Against the yen, the euro was flat at 117.81 yen against 11.73 in late New York. \par \par \uc2  The Swiss franc, now seen as a "safe haven", rose to 1.5611 francs per dollar against 1.5627 in late U.S. trade. \par \par \uc2  "Nobody wants to trade today because they don't want to have open positions when the Japan-Tunisia (soccer) match begins and also since it is ahead of the G7 meeting this weekend," said Yoshiyasu Naruse, senior manager of the treasury sales section at HSBC in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  A crucial World Cup match between Japan and Tunisia kicks off at 3:30 p.m. (0630 GMT). DOLLAR YET TO FIND TREND \par \par \uc2  From a mid-term perspective, some players said the dollar, after slipping more than eight percent against the yen since its recent high of 133.84 yen reached on April 2, may have hit its low. \par \par \uc2  But technical analysts said it was too early to make such assessments. \par \par \uc2  "There is no clear trend in the dollar/yen right now," said Osamu Takashima, technical analyst at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi. \par \par \uc2  "It is still locked in ranges between 122.82 and 126.36 but I personally think there is a bigger possibility of the dollar testing the upside to 127 and 128 yen rather than 123 yen." \par \par \uc2  A session low of 126.36 yen marked on March 7 is seen as a key resistance level during a test upside while May 30's recent low of 122.82 yen is expected to be a firm support line. \par \par \uc2  "126.36 will not be an easy level to break but if the dollar does gain upward momentum, it will be cleared," Takashima said. \par \par \uc2  JAPAN WORRIES \par \par \uc2  Some analysts said the recent slip of the yen against the greenback was due to worries that Japan's economic recovery may not be as strong as once hoped for. \par \par \uc2  "Compared to when some were saying the GDP (gross domestic product) growth would be two digits for the first quarter, I think such strong expectations (for strong recovery) have disappeared," said Takashi Yamanaka, economist at UFJ Bank. \par \par \uc2  "After looking at the conditions of the United States, I think some are getting cautious." \par \par \uc2  Japan's January-March GDP rose 1.4 percent from the previous quarter. \par \par \uc2  But most players said the limited nature of the yen's gains was due to repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The dollar's floor has been rising recently and this is due to \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese authorities," HSBC's Naruse said. "The Japanese economy is not the centre of focus right now. \par \par \uc2  "For example, I don't think the yen has been reacting to Nikkei prices recently," he added. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the focus of the market will be on the U.S. economy, such as the University of Michigan consumer sentiment index data due later on Friday. Economists expect a downtick in June's preliminary index, to 96.8 from 96.9 in May. \par \par \uc2  U.S. May industrial production and April business inventories will also be watched, traders said. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 0512 GMT. All data taken from Reuters with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at 2130 GMT. \par \par \uc2   Last     US Close  % Chg  YTD %   2001 Cls \par \par \uc2   13 Jun. \par \par \uc2   ---------------------------------------- \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Euro/dlr       0.9428   0.9427   +0.01    +5.88   0.8904\par  Dlr/yen        124.84   124.86   -0.02    -5.16   131.63\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Euro/yen    117.76   117.73   +0.03    +0.44   117.24\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Dlr/swiss      1.5635   1.5627   +0.05    -5.78   1.6594\par  Stg/dlr        1.4729   1.4720   +0.06    +1.29   1.4541\par  Can/dlr        1.5393   1.5388   +0.03    -3.29   1.5917\par  Aus/dlr        0.5663   0.5666   -0.05   +10.91   0.5106\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Euro/swiss  1.4737   1.4737   +0.00    -0.29   1.4780\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Euro/stg    0.6400   0.6408   -0.12    +4.52   0.6123\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  All \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  