\par\par \b \uc2  CHARTING ASIA: USD/JPY Recovery In Danger Near-Term \b0\par\par\uc2  By Andrew Torchia \par \uc2 910 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Juni 2002\par 06:12\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  A Dow Jones Newswires Column \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The recent recovery of the dollar against the yen is in danger of losing steam, at least in the short term. \par \par \uc2  Around midday in Asia, USD/JPY has been aggressively testing important support at 124.70 - its short-term uptrend line from late May - for the past several hours. Even a strong earthquake that shook Tokyo this morning - and apparently failed to cause injuries or significant damage - didn't interrupt the test. \par \par \uc2  If the support holds and USD/JPY rebounds, it can be expected to test and quite possibly break resistance at its downtrend line from the start of April, which comes in today around 125.20. A clean break would be a major positive signal for USD/JPY, indicating its medium-term downtrend had ended. \par \par \uc2  But if the support breaks, and the break is confirmed by trading below 124.70 at the end of the global day, the rosy scenario described above will be destroyed - or at least postponed. \par \par \uc2  USD/JPY would then test minor support at 124.59 (a peak at the end of May) and probably break it, heading down for a test of key support around 123.50. That's where its long-term, four-point uptrend line dating back to September 2000 currently comes in. \par \par \uc2  USD/JPY's fall from a high of 125.89 yesterday certainly had some of the characteristics of a reversal of a short-term uptrend; on the daily candlestick chart, for example, it formed a bearish engulfing pattern. So a break of 124.70 support looks possible. \par \par \uc2  There appear to have been two "news" triggers for the reversal. One is last night's worse-than-expected U.S. May retail sales. But that's not a very convincing trigger; at this stage signs of renewed U.S. economic weakness are damaging not only Wall Street but stock markets around the globe, including Tokyo, where the Nikkei (down 1.7% this morning) continues to tumble after its technical breakdown at the end of last week. If the U.S. economic recovery does fade, Japan's export-led recovery will suffer at least as much. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The more likely trigger is news of opposition within the U.S. to the\par Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's recent \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to support USD/JPY. On Wednesday, 24\par U.S. Congressmen sent a letter to U.S. Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill\par complaining about the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and asking O'Neill to raise the issue\par at this week's Group of Seven meeting in Canada.\par    Then a few hours before Asian markets opened today,   U.S. Treasury\par Undersecretary John Taylor told reporters that Japan policymakers\par should focus on steps to raise productivity and long-term economic\par growth rather than relying simply on a weaker \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to boost\par exports; his comments were taken by some as veiled criticism of BOJ\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The changes needed for sustainable long-term growth in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are\par linked to productivity growth, not \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates," Taylor said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Since BOJ \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was crucial in lifting USD/JPY off its low of\par 122.86 and launching its short-term uptrend last month, the idea of the\par U.S. pressuring \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  not to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  again has inevitably encouraged\par the market to test its downside again.\par    If the test succeeds in breaking the September 2000 uptrend line at\par 123.50, it will be immensely bearish for   USD/JPY; the market would\par be pointed down to its September low of 115.84, with initial supports\par at 122.23 (the 38.2% retracement of the upmove from November 1999 to\par January this year), then 119.84 (a peak in January 2001) and 118.23\par (the 50% retracement).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Will the key support break? I don't think so; the BOJ's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par so far appears to have been designed to protect the trendline, and the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is unlikely to change its approach now and allow a slide\par that would undo all it's achieved so far.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Senior Japanese Finance Ministry official Haruhiko Kuroda warned this\par morning that Japan was ready to take "decisive" action to block\par excessive yen strength, which was a warning to the market not to assume\par the BOJ would back down (though he would say that).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par And it may be wrong to read too much into Taylor's comments. He was\par responding to concern expressed by some U.S. politicians and\par industrialists about the dollar's strength and its impact on U.S.\par exports, but that doesn't mean the U.S. has decided it will press Japan\par to let USD/JPY slide, which would ultimately hurt Japan and could risk\par an uncontrolled, global collapse of the dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par BOJ \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was very effective last month in protecting the\par long-term uptrend line, and if necessary the BOJ will probably \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par below 124.00 to protect it again.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Another bounce from this trendline would set the stage for a break of\par the medium-term downtrend line, probably within the next fortnight. That\par would point USD/JPY up to resistance at 126.53, the trough in March, and\par stronger resistance around the 127.00 level.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par So the medium-term outlook for USD/JPY isn't bearish; the drop since\par yesterday, and Taylor's comments, have at the most probably just delayed\par USD/JPY's recovery by a week or two.\par -by Andrew Torchia; (65) 6415-4013; andrew.torchiadowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1181 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020614dy6e0006u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
