\par\par \b \uc2  Less vibrant U.S. economy a worry for G7 partners. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Glenn Somerville \par \uc2 915 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Juni 2002\par 05:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WASHINGTON, June 13 (Reuters) - U.S. Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill joins finance chiefs from other rich industrial nations in Halifax this weekend to mull a global economy whose mightiest engine is not firing on all cylinders. \par \par \uc2  U.S. data out on Thursday showed a stumble in May retail sales while the once-indomitable U.S. dollar has been weakening - enough to raise questions among Group of Seven finance ministers about whether the U.S. expansion is low on fuel. \par \par \uc2  "One concern for the G7 ministers is that a global economic rebound has been largely predicated upon, and helped by, a revival in U.S. economic activity, so if U.S. growth is slowing there are ramifications for the rest of the world including for Japan and Europe," said economist Sung Won Sohn of Wells Fargo Bank in Minneapolis. \par \par \uc2  O'Neill told the National Federation of Independent Business on Thursday "the fundamentals of our economy continue to be strong," but conceded there was a mood of wariness about the durability of the U.S. recovery from last year's mild recession. \par \par \uc2  "Too many people are still out of work and we need to see more business investment to maintain the recovery," the U.S. Treasury chief said. \par \par \uc2  O'Neill and fellow ministers from Britain, Canada, France, Germany, Italy and Japan meet June 14-15 in the East Coast Canadian city. Part of their task is to help set an agenda for the June 26-28 gathering of heads of government from the Group of Eight, which includes Russia, at a remote Western Canadian resort in Kananaskis, Alberta. \par \par \uc2  That means some continuing discussion about broad issues like how to crimp the ability of groups that sponsor terrorism to use the global banking system to move funds around. \par \par \uc2  DOLLARS IN THE CORRIDOR \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors are not attending the Halifax meeting, so \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues are not on any formal agenda though corridor discussions are possible - and perhaps likely with the dollar sliding against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The other ministers may very well want to talk about the value of the dollar," Sohn said, "After all, there could be a double-whammy for other nations, and especially for Japan, if their exports slow because that's what they've been pinning their hopes for recovery on." \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury Under Secretary for International Affairs John Taylor told a briefing for reporters ahead of the G7 session the United States had strong first-quarter economic growth and that European economies were "beginning to pick up with a bit of a lag." \par \par \uc2  But he said that Japan, while it logged growth in the first quarter, still needed to come to grips with a mountain of nonperforming loans at its banks and to get price deflation, which saps the value of assets, under control. \par \par \uc2  Taylor said it was hard to see how Japan, Asia's largest economy, could ever get a long-running economic recovery in place unless it makes necessary reforms. He said G7 participants expected to hear more proposals from Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa for reinvigorating growth. \par \par \uc2  In response to a question, Taylor cited past remarks by O'Neill that Japan needed to take steps to increase its economic productivity and said that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets was not the way to effect lasting changes. \par \par \uc2  A cheaper U.S. dollar makes it more costly for American consumers to buy Japanese-made cars and other imported goods. That makes it a threat to other countries' ability to sell into American markets and - if a months' long slide steepens - a potential worry for a global recovery. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets at least four times since late May, seeking to keep the yen from rapidly appreciating, raising hackles among U.S. lawmakers. \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, 24 U.S. legislators sent a letter to O'Neill saying American jobs were being put at risk and asking that he express disapproval of Japan's manipulation at the weekend G7 meeting. That is unlikely, however, since O'Neill does not have a bilateral meeting scheduled with Japan's Shiokawa. \par \par \uc2  ARGENTINA ON THE TABLE \par \par \uc2  Aside from their own concerns, the G7 ministers have a broader agenda including how to guide troubled Argentina back to economic health and hopefully prevent its ills from infecting other Latin American economies. \par \par \uc2  A British Treasury official said on Thursday there likely would be discussions about Argentina's "severe difficulties." \par \par \uc2  Canadian Finance Minister John Manley, host of the meeting, said the group may discuss Brazil's economic problems in the context of Argentina's more serious financial woes, but that he was not certain the group would have a particular message for the Brazilians at the moment. \par \par \uc2  There has been mounting concern that Argentina's woes could spread to neighboring countries, but Taylor played down the idea that Brazil was at growing risk of such contagion. \par \par \uc2  "I think fundamentals in Brazil are good with respect to their inflation and their budget," Taylor said. \par \par \uc2  Argentina is seeking billions of dollars in loans from the International Monetary Fund to try to get its crippled economy back on track while financial markets worry that Brazil's economy also is moving onto shaky ground. \par \par \uc2  An IMF mission landed in Argentina on Thursday to discuss the possibility of resuming aid after a six-month freeze, which could open the door to other aid sources and help stop contagion to neighboring countries. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020614dy6e006bt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
