\par\par \b \uc2  Firm Yen Raises  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Specter But Policy In Doubt \b0\par\par\uc2  By John Hardy \par \uc2 686 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Juni 2002\par 22:34\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  OF DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- With the dollar under pressure again Friday in response to a terrorist bombing in Pakistan,  disappointing U.S. economic data and another sharp drop in stocks, traders are once again on alert for the Bank of Japan - concerned that a stronger yen could derail \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's fragile recovery - to resume its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The dollar briefly dipped below Y124.00 during the New York session and hovered just above that level through late afternoon trading. Analysts say this renders it very possible that the BOJ would step in to the market if the dollar continues to slide when Asian trading starts up Monday. \par \par \uc2  "I think they'll make a token \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  around Y123.70," said Glenn Stevens, managing director at GAIN Capital in Warren NJ, suggesting that such a move is essential to head off a major new step higher for the yen. \par \par \uc2  But coming out of a meeting of finance ministers from the Group of Seven this weekend in Canada, other market observers believe \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be wary of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  again, particularly in the wake of comments from a senior U.S. Treasury official suggesting that the Japanese authorities should focus more on stimulating domestic economic growth and less on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The changes needed for sustainable long-term growth in  \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  are linked to productivity growth, not \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates," said U.S. Treasury Undersecretary John Taylor ahead of the G7 meeting in Halifax, Nova Scotia. \par \par \uc2  Taylor's words were seen by some as a thinly-veiled attack on Tokyo's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets - which it has carried out on four occasions in the any weeks at an estimated cost of $20 billion - to prevent the yen from strengthening too much. \par \par \uc2  "This was a very blunt statement...(showing that) the U.S. is becoming more hostile to \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's attitude," on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said Hans Redeker, head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at BNP Paribas in London. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Remarkably Comfortable With Policy\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But U.S. analysts doubt that Taylor's comments signify any real U.S.\par opposition to Japanese policy.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I don't think this will have any impact," said Jason Bonanca,\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Credit Suisse First Boston in New York."The U.S.\par has shown a level of comfort (with Japanese policy) that's quite\par remarkable,"  he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Keeping the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  threat at the forefront of traders' minds are\par renewed comments from Japanese policy makers, reiterating their\par determination to continue fighting  the strength of the yen. "Japan's\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy hasn't changed whatsoever," said Haruhiko\par Kuroda, Japan's vice minister for international affairs. "We will take\par decisive action against excessive rises in the yen."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par So traders continue to assume that the BOJ is closely monitoring\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets, with some suggesting the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could make its\par presence felt not far below current levels. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  analysts also\par believe Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  - the country's guardian of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign\par exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy - wouldn't hesitate to order further \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  if\par faced with rapid appreciation of the yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Their tactic is to look at sharp moves and smooth them  out," said\par CSFB's Bonanaca. "They're not level sensitive - but they would be very\par concerned if the yen approached Y120."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Late Friday in New York, the dollar was at Y124.14, above its lows for\par the day at Y123.98, but well lower than Y124.90 late Thursday in New\par York.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par There is a chance, however, that the BOJ might not feel compelled to\par take action to weaken the yen, given that market trends may be starting\par to point to a weaker yen in any case. The driving force behind recent\par yen gains - a major rally in Japanese stocks - now looks a bit shaky,\par which analysts say is reducing the market's interest in buying\par yen-assets. On Friday, the Nikkei 225 surrendered 2.0% to close at\par 10920.63, below 11000 for the first time in  two months.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By  John Hardy, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-2122;\par john.hardy@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020020614dy6e00bvv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal