\par\par \b \uc2  Negative U.S. Economic Data Puts Pressure on the Dollar \b0\par\par\uc2  By John Parry \par \uc2 978 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Juni 2002\par 01:02\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -- The dollar slid lower against the yen Thursday but was modestly higher against the euro, after trading in fairly wide ranges as market players digested the impact of decidedly negative U.S. economic data. \par \par \uc2  Weighed down by the dual effects of economic reports and -- partly resulting from that -- a retreating stock market, the dollar slipped through some key levels against the yen and the euro Thursday, but then pared some of its losses. \par \par \uc2  Late Thursday, the dollar was at 124.87, fairly close to its intraday lows and quite steeply down from 125.67 late Wednesday in New York. The euro was at 94.25 U.S. cents, moderately down from 94.37 cents late Wednesday in New York, having also traded in a fairly wide range, with a global session high of 94.73 cents and session low of 93.98 cents. \par \par \uc2  Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was at 1.5645 Swiss francs, up from 1.5650 francs late Wednesday. Sterling was at $1.4711, compared with $1.4710, stabilizing after its strong performance earlier this week partly because of Rupert Murdoch's newspapers planned campaign against sterling's entry to the euro. \par \par \uc2  U.S. retail sales for May fell 0.9%, a much sharper decline than expected, leading some economists to postpone their expectations for Federal Reserve interest rate increases until close to the end of the year. \par \par \uc2  The reading was "pretty bad and that's reinforcing the notion of a slow recovery, hurting stocks," whose fortunes appear decidedly "tied in" with the dollar's, said Larry Brickman, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and interest rate strategist at Banc of America Securities in New York. \par \par \uc2  In peripheral \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Brazilian real managed to garner some breathing space, bouncing back from nine-month lows around 2.795 Brazilian reals to the dollar it had plumbed Wednesday. The real was cheered by the Brazilian government's confirmation that it plans to draw on some $10 billion in International Monetary Fund credit lines and to undertake fiscal measures with the aim of calming domestic markets. Late in New York, the real was off its highs for the day, but still sharply higher on the session, at around BRR2.6595 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  But with presidential elections looming in October and concerns about Brazil's ability to finance its debt, analysts question whether the real can maintain its comeback. "Markets are going to be on edge because of the political uncertainty and.. the big question: will Brazil have difficulty rolling over its short term debt," said Paresh Upadhyaya, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Putnam Investments in Boston. \par \par \uc2  Among the major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the euro failed to make another assault on the 17-month high of 95.05 cents it had reached Wednesday, confirming the view among some strategists that the single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent upward momentum may have reached a plateau. Holding the euro back were some hawkish statements from the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a monthly report. The ECB said the outlook for prices was less satisfactory than a few months ago and that recent wage agreements -- referring implicitly to German trade union IG Metall's deal with employers -- were a cause for concern. \par \par \uc2  "There are increasing signs of fatigue among euro bulls," at least over the near term, said Alan Ruskin, research director at 4Cast in New York. \par \par \uc2  Perhaps fueling this, ECB President Wim Duisenberg acknowledged Thursday that the euro zone's growth potential may never catch up to that of the U.S. owing to the diversity of cultures and languages in Europe. "Yes it's true the euro has rallied," said Marc Chandler, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at HSBC in New York, but Mr. Duisenberg's comments "are consistent with the belief out there that the euro is like a closed-end mutual fund that will always sell below its net asset value." \par \par \uc2  Several economists are certainly turning more bullish on the euro. Goldman Sach's head of economic research Jim O'Neill -- a perennial euro bull -- on Thursday raised the bank's 12-month target to $1.06, from $1.03 previously. But there's still a sense that the euro's latest rally is running out of gas. \par \par \uc2  Some longer term global funds based outside Europe are becoming "actually quite nervous" about continuing to invest on a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  unhedged basis in euro-denominated assets with the euro already at quite high levels against the dollar, Mr. Ruskin said. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the dollar slipped once again below the psychologically important 125.00 mark. Although that slightly increases traders fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in an effort to weaken the yen, that's still some way from the so-called 'discomfort zone" where the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , acting via the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  during the past three weeks. \par \par \uc2  At these levels, the major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are likely to raise few hackles among finance ministers from the Group of Seven leading industrial nations convening in Halifax, Canada, this weekend. Despite the persistent weakness of the dollar and some warnings that it might be in for a sharp decline, many analysts believe the U.S. government is quite comfortable with the dollar's gradual descent, as it enables U.S. exporters to be somewhat more competitive, without importing strong inflationary pressures. \par \par \uc2  Supporting this, Federal Reserve Bank of Philadelphia President Anthony Santomero said Thursday that the sharp depreciation of the dollar over the past several weeks should help the U.S. recovery by boosting trade. "At these levels, there will be some long-term positive impact on our trade balance," Mr. Santomero told reporters on the sidelines of a conference hosted by the Austrian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Write to John Parry at john.parry@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 2002 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020613dy6d007vd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
