\par\par \b \uc2  BOJ Econ Report -4: Still Concerned Over Overseas Econs \b0\par\par\uc2 393 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Juni 2002\par 07:48\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  The BOJ maintained a cautious note over the strength and sustainability of overseas economic recovery. "There are also various uncertain factors concerning the outlook for overseas economies, in particular the U.S. economy," the report said. \par \par \uc2  As for private demand, a key engine for a sustainable economic recovery, the BOJ said, "Private companies continue to reduce fixed costs. As a result, employee incomes are still clearly falling." \par \par \uc2  The BOJ also said the tempo of net export growth will very likely moderate as private companies overseas are expected to stop increasing inventories. \par \par \uc2  Industrial output may rise temporarily, but the pace of rise will likely become more moderate, the BOJ said. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ cited the recovery in manufacturing as a favorable sign for the still-fragile economy, though it added it will take more time for that recovery to permeate through to non-manufacturers and private households due to the persistent structural pressure weighing on the economy. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ's assessment of prices for June remained unchanged from May. "Consumer prices are expected to keep falling moderately for the time being, and weak wages may add to the downward pressure on service prices," the BOJ said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will keep its quantitative easing policy in place until core CPI stabilizes at zero or higher. \par \par \uc2  In addition, the BOJ said that the yen's rise against the U.S. dollar reflected the overall downtrend in the dollar, though rates have been mixed since late May due mainly to yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese monetary authorities. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets on June 4, selling yen to curb the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent rise for the fourth time in less than two weeks as authorities try to stop a resurgent yen from damping a nascent economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ said in its report that it will closely monitor developments in financial markets, including \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, and their impact on the Japanese economy. \par \par \uc2  The overall upgrade to its economic assessment this month is unlikely to lead to a fine-tuning of the BOJ's easy policy. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has said it is unlikely to consider such a move until it sees signs of a recovery in production, income and personal consumption. \par \par \uc2  -By Hiroshi Inoue, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; hiroshi.inoue@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020613dy6d0009h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
