\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar recovers from 17-month low against euro. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Christina Fincher \par \uc2 720 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Juni 2002\par 14:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 13 (Reuters) - The dollar recovered its poise on Thursday, clawing back a cent from recent 17-month lows against the euro as investors bet a recovery on Wall Street would continue. \par \par \uc2  Futures markets were pointing to a firm open on Wall Street, indicating major U.S. stocks would extend Wednesday's bounce of more than one percent. \par \par \uc2  Investors were also reluctant to extend the greenback's recent hefty losses ahead of a batch of U.S. economic data later in the day. \par \par \uc2  "There continues to be a high correlation between stocks and the dollar," said Michael Klawitter, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at WestLB. \par \par \uc2  "Investors are already very short dollars and long euros, which means the euro has found it difficult to move above $0.95." \par \par \uc2  The dollar's performance has been closely correlated with U.S. stocks in recent days, with Wall Street seen as a barometer of investor confidence in corporate America. \par \par \uc2  The euro fell briefly below $0.94 in European trade, easing from Wednesday's lofty peaks above $0.95. \par \par \uc2  The dollar shuttled in a narrow range around 125.50 yen, having clawed back almost three yen from six-month lows hit in late May. \par \par \uc2  "Wall Street's firmer tone is helping the dollar," said Mitul Kotecha, global head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Credit Agricole Indosuez. \par \par \uc2  U.S. DATA AWAITED \par \par \uc2  Dealers said U.S. retail sales, producer price and weekly jobless data at 1230 GMT would be closely watched for clues on the health of the world's biggest economy. \par \par \uc2  The dollar has been under heavy pressure since early April as concerns over the strength of the U.S. recovery and U.S. accounting standards have dimmed the once-unrivalled appeal of U.S. assets. \par \par \uc2  U.S. retail sales figures are expected to show consumer spending cooled last month and could put renewed pressure on fragile U.S. equities and the greenback. \par \par \uc2  A Reuters poll of economists expects U.S. retail sales data to show a drop of 0.3 percent in May, excluding automobile sales, after a 1.2 percent gain in April. \par \par \uc2  The market is also keeping an eye on weekly jobless claims - forecast to stay near the 400,000 level, which often signals a stagnant labour market. \par \par \uc2  Against such a background, analysts said worries over U.S. corporate profitability would continue. \par \par \uc2  "U.S. stocks still look unattractive compared with elsewhere and divestment out of U.S. into the euro zone and Asian assets will continue," said Steve Barrow, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bear Stearns. \par \par \uc2  EURO TAKES BREATHER \par \par \uc2  The euro also eased against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , shedding almost a yen from the previous session's five-month highs above 119 yen. \par \par \uc2  "The market needs new impetus to push the euro higher," said Kotecha at Credit Agricole Indosuez. \par \par \uc2  The euro showed little reaction to earlier data which showed French industry output grew at a robust pace in April, while Spanish consumer prices rose more than expected in May. \par \par \uc2  The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reinforced the message that an interest rate rise is on the cards by releasing internal forecasts that put euro zone inflation above its two percent ceiling this and possibly next year. \par \par \uc2  By contrast, recent comments from the Federal Reserve have indicated that U.S. interest rates could remain at current low levels for some months to come. \par \par \uc2  COMPLAINTS ON \b \highlight22\uc2 JAPAN INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  Although markets reacted little, traders took note of a letter sent to U.S. Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill by 24 U.S. lawmakers complaining about what they called Japan's meddling with \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates. \par \par \uc2  Japan has sold its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on at least four days since late May, concerned that the yen's strength against the dollar could harm the country's fragile export-led recovery. \par \par \uc2  The lawmakers asked O'Neill to convey their disapproval at a meeting of Group of Seven finance ministers to be held in Halifax, Canada, on June 14-15. \par \par \uc2  However, a British Treasury official said on Thursday that G7 finance ministers were unlikely to hold detailed discussions on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  "We will be talking about economic growth, trade, and some people may talk about current account balances," the Treasury official told reporters. \par \par \uc2  "I would be surprised if there were any detailed discussions on exchange rates." \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020613dy6d00ovk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
