\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar makes minor recovery vs euro, eyes US data. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Natsuko Waki \par \uc2 631 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Juni 2002\par 10:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 13 (Reuters) - The dollar held above the previous day's 17-month lows against the euro on Thursday, garnering support from a late bounce on Wall Street and holding its breath ahead of a key U.S. retail sales report due later. \par \par \uc2  Economists expect the report is likely to show consumer spending cooled last month, which could bring renewed pressure on fragile U.S. equities and the greenback. \par \par \uc2  U.S. stocks rallied by more than one percent on Wednesday, putting aside worries about corporate profits and a stream of accounting scandals. \par \par \uc2  "We have to see how stocks would respond to the (sales) report. Recently we have had good U.S. data, yet stocks look poor," said Steve Barrow, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bear Stearns. \par \par \uc2  "U.S. stocks still look unattractive compared with elsewhere. Divestment out of U.S. into the eurozone and Asian assets will continue and I think that will bring the dollar down going forward." \par \par \uc2  At 0740 GMT, the dollar was steady versus the euro at $0.9422, having stumbled to a 17-month trough beyond $0.95 on Wednesday. Against the yen, it stood close to a three-week high of 125.87 set on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The euro trimmed its gains against the yen after hitting a five-month peak of 119.27 set on Wednesday, showing muted reaction to French manufacturing output data which confirmed the recovery trend seen since January. \par \par \uc2  A Reuters poll of economists expect U.S. retail sales data to show a drop of 0.3 percent, excluding automobile sales, after a 1.2 percent gain in April. The market is also keeping an eye on weekly jobless claims - forecast to stay near the 400,000 level, which often signals a stagnant labour market. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say weaker than expected data could further stave off the possibility of a Federal Reserve interest rate hike in coming months. \par \par \uc2  With somewhat tame inflation, the producer price index is likely to have edged up only 0.1 percent in May after April's fall of 0.2 percent. \par \par \uc2  COMPLAINTS AHEAD OF G7 \par \par \uc2  Although the markets reacted little, Tokyo traders took note of a letter sent to U.S. Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill by 24 U.S. lawmakers complaining about what they called Japan's meddling with \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates. \par \par \uc2  "Ahead of the U.S. interim election (in November), we're expecting to hear many complaints about \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . These comments should have a psychological impact in limiting heavy dollar-buying," a Japanese trust bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  House Small Business Committee Chairman Donald Manzullo, who headed the committee discussing the issue, called on O'Neill to put pressure on countries like Japan and China to stop manipulating their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar's value must be determined by market forces, not government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . I call on our government to step back and let the dollar correct itself," he said in a statement. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market at least four times since late May and is estimated to have bought over two trillion yen worth of dollars for yen. \par \par \uc2  The lawmakers asked O'Neill to convey their disapproval at a meeting of Group of Seven finance ministers to be held in Halifax, Canada, on June 14-15. \par \par \uc2  Japan's top financial diplomat, Haruhiko Kuroda, said on Wednesday \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates and monetary policy would not be on the agenda at Halifax but a Canadian finance official did not rule out the possibility, saying \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues often come up in G7 meetings. \par \par \uc2  Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said the country's current account surplus in April rose by 21.7 percent from a year earlier to 1.085 trillion yen ($8.66 billion). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020613dy6d00gdk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
