\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 2-Rising Japanese exports prompt upbeat BOJ report. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Yoko Nishikawa \par \uc2 800 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Juni 2002\par 08:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 13 (Reuters) - The Bank of Japan upgraded its economic assessment for the fourth month in a row on Thursday, citing an export-driven recovery in output. \par \par \uc2  That was underscored earlier in the day by balance of payments data showing healthy export growth. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  warned, however, that the pace of the recovery would slow because the economic upturn in Japan's global trading partners is moderating - a reminder that consumption in Japan itself remains lacklustre. \par \par \uc2  "Japan's economy shows signs of stabilising with a distinct increase in exports and a pickup in production, although domestic private demand remains weak," the BOJ monthly report said. \par \par \uc2  Fuelled by strong exports of cars and electronic goods, Japan emerged from recession in the January-March quarter, clocking growth of an annualised 5.7 percent from the previous quarter - its best in two years. \par \par \uc2  Many economists, like the BOJ, have forecast a slowdown from that heady pace, or even a so-called double-dip recession, as the global rebound levels off and a resurgent yen squeezes a windfall price advantage for Japanese exporters. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ report came out soon after balance of payments data for April showed exports rose from a year earlier for the first time in more than a year, thanks to a pickup in demand by Japan's Asian neighbours. \par \par \uc2  Also backing up the BOJ's analysis on Thursday were revised industrial production figures which confirmed that output rose 0.2 percent in April from March, the third month of gains. \par \par \uc2  Financial markets paid little attention to the data and the dollar was hardly changed, hovering at around 125.70 yen in afternoon Tokyo trade. \par \par \uc2  EXPORT GROWTH \par \par \uc2  The surplus in Japan's current account, the broadest measure of trade in goods and services, grew 21.7 percent from a year earlier in April, the seventh straight month of increase, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said. \par \par \uc2  The 1.085 trillion yen ($8.66 billion) surplus was slightly below a median forecast of 1.19 trillion yen in a poll of 10 economists by Reuters last week. \par \par \uc2  Looking at a breakdown, economists said the rise in exports stood out, particularly as the income account surplus - the return on portfolio and direct investments abroad - logged its first decline in 16 months. \par \par \uc2  The data showed exports rose 1.3 percent year-on-year to 4.169 trillion yen, the first rise in 13 months. \par \par \uc2  Imports were down for a ninth month, by 3.2 percent to 3.164 trillion yen, reflecting a lagging pickup in domestic demand. But that decline slowed from a double-digit pace in recent months. \par \par \uc2  A ministry official said growth in exports to Asia was the driving force behind the rising surplus, adding that a fall in the income account was due to a one-off technical factor. \par \par \uc2  "There is no change in the overall trend. We think the trade surplus is entering a recovery trend...and the current account surplus will stay on a firm rising trend," he added. \par \par \uc2  But Japan's reliance on exports, taking advantage of a weaker yen, has touched raw nerves with many of its trade partners. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill was urged by 24 House lawmakers in a letter on Wednesday to press Japan to stop manipulating \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates, Jiji news agency reported. \par \par \uc2  "We ask you to strongly communicate to the government of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  the U.S. government's disapproval of direct Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets," it quoted them as saying. \par \par \uc2  Compounding the problem, Japanese monetary authorities have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market at least four times since late May to curb a rise in the yen and are estimated to have spent over two trillion yen ($15.97 billion) to buy dollars for yen. \par \par \uc2  The lawmakers asked O'Neill to convey the U.S. disapproval at a meeting of the Group of Seven finance ministers to be held in Halifax, Canada, on June 14-15, Jiji said. \par \par \uc2  Earlier this year, the yen fell to 3 = year lows around 135 to the dollar. It has since rebounded by about seven percent. \par \par \uc2  Koichi Ono, an economist at Daiwa Institute of Research, said the magnitude of the recent rise in the yen was not yet a worry. \par \par \uc2  "I am not concerned that it will hurt the overall trend in exports," he said. \par \par \uc2  "Unless there is a rapid rise in the yen, the income balance and trade balance will continue to rise, thus keeping the current account surplus on a clear and steady rise." \par \par \uc2  According to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's "tankan" survey of corporate sentiment released in April, large manufacturers on average set a dollar rate of 124.21 yen for their business plans for the year to next March. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020613dy6d005k5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
