\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar holds gains, Japan April surplus stays high. \b0\par\par\uc2 333 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Juni 2002\par 03:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  0140 GMT - The dollar retained its overnight gains against the yen and euro, helped by gains on Wall Street. - \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier said Japan's current account surplus for April was at seasonally adjusted 1.1581 trillion yen ($9.25 billion), down from March's 1.5475 trillion yen but still at high levels thanks to dollar's rise versus yen, which pushes up exporters' income in yen terms. - Large current account surplus of Japan and bloating U.S. deficit are causing some tension among U.S. lawmakers and exporters. - On Wednesday, twenty-four U.S. House lawmakers urged Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill to press Japan to stop manipulating \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates, a Japanese news agency reported. - They said that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are unacceptable as they have been effective in stopping the markets' moves to weaken the dollar. - The lawmakers also asked O'Neill to convey the U.S. \par \par \uc2   disapproval at a meeting of Group of Seven finance ministers to be held in Halifax, Canada, on June 14-15, the report said. - \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders are sensitive to remarks related to U.S. dollar policy as U.S. monetary officials have remained silent on dollar's recent falls and Bank of Japan's attempt to prop it up. - Some interpret lack of response by U.S. regarding BOJ's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  as tacit disapproval of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's move, because a strong dollar further hurts U.S. exporters, while it boosts export income of U.S. trading partners. - Dollar quoted at 125.75/80 yen, up a bit from 125.69 in late New York on Wednesday. - Euro quoted at $0.9441/46, firmer than $0.9432 in late New York. - Euro fell as low as $0.9429 overnight after rising to a 17-month high of $0.9507 in late Tokyo on Wednesday. - Euro also dived to around 118.20 yen overnight after rising to high of 119.27 yen in New York. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020613dy6d003v1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
