\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Rallies Against Rivals On Late Surge in U.S. Stocks \b0\par\par\uc2  By John Hardy \par \uc2 904 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Juni 2002\par 01:04\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -- Although the euro touched a new 17-month peak Wednesday, the dollar fought back -- taking heart from a late rally in stocks and moving higher on the day against both the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the yen. \par \par \uc2  The Brazilian real meanwhile, continued to slide, hitting its lowest level since Sept. 21, 2001, as fears that Brazil may be en route to a potential debt crisis. The real steadily lost ground before ending at 2.795 Brazilian reals to the dollar, the worst finish since its historic low of 2.835 reals on Sept. 21. \par \par \uc2  Market players expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to take some sort of action to halt the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decline Thursday, when Governor Arminio Fraga is scheduled to return from a trip to Washington. The real has lost more than 10% of its value so far this month. Traders said part of the weakening is related to corporate and retail deals to send capital overseas on election jitters. \par \par \uc2  In other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the euro's initial rally, which propelled it to 95.05 U.S. cents during London trading for the first time since January 2001, quickly ran out of steam, with analysts suggesting that the common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's five-month rally against the dollar might falter. \par \par \uc2  "I'm not sure there's any fundamentals underpinning to the euro's rise," said Steve Pearson, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at HBOS Treasury Services in London, suggesting that overseas investors will be cautious about adding to their euro positions. \par \par \uc2  But at the same time, the dollar's gains also weren't that convincing. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  did take some heart from a jump by equities into positive territory late in the New York day, but the erratic performance of stocks during the session did little to erase fears of further weakness, which in turn is expected to further weigh down the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "There's been a rapid unwinding of the main view heading into 2002, which was you weren't going to have blockbuster gains in stocks, but returns would be positive," said Jim McCormick, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Lehman Brothers in New York. "But stock markets are continuing to move lower." \par \par \uc2  "The microscope has got much tougher this year on the U.S.," he said. "There used to be this ability to shrug off any bad news. But now we've moved into a market that's having a hard time embracing any good news." \par \par \uc2  The International Monetary Fund, which has traditionally highlighted the unsustainability of the U.S. current account deficit as a key risk to the global recovery, also seemed to read the minds of stock and dollar investors Wednesday. In its latest Financial Stability Report, the IMF said that uncertainty over a recovery of corporate profits and business investment in the U.S. and Europe remains the greatest potential risk to the global economy. \par \par \uc2  "In the period ahead, the level of corporate profitability will significantly influence capital expenditure, which so far is the missing component in the current recovery," the IMF said. \par \par \uc2  With markets seeking guidance on the next direction of major \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pairs, U.S. economic data Wednesday offered little help. The Labor Department reported that import prices were unchanged in May, benefiting from easing inflationary pressures. The latest Federal Reserve Beige Book indicated that the U.S. economy grew at a moderate rate in late April and early May, broadly confirming that the economy is continuing its rebound from recession, but not going anywhere fast. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's outlook against the yen seems somewhat brighter, however. With dealers continuing to worry about the threat of further Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yen again weakened under broad-based selling pressure, with the dollar pushing to a three-week high at 125.87 yen, and the euro touching a five-month peak at 119.29 yen. \par \par \uc2  In late New York trading Wednesday, the euro was at 94.32 cents, down from 94.87 cents in London and below 94.88 cents late Tuesday in New York. The dollar was at 125.71 yen up from 125.35 yen in London and above 125.34 yen late Tuesday in New York. The dollar was also at 1.5660 Swiss francs, up from 1.5540 francs late Tuesday. Sterling was at $1.4711, down from $1.4722 late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  With the euro relatively strong, traders report that gains in the euro against the yen have given support to the dollar, allowing it to establish a solid beachhead above 125.00 yen. Pro-yen sentiment by offshore investors is also waning rapidly, analysts say, with Japanese equities markets beginning to lose their appeal. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, sterling eased slightly in New York trading, after touching a session high at $1.4754 in overseas trading, its highest level against the dollar since October, 2001. The pound has been underpinned by hopes of a near-term U.K. rate hike, following the strong manufacturing and industrial output data Tuesday, as well as reports that Rupert Murdoch's newspaper group will oppose a pro-euro campaign ahead of any referendum, which could cause the pound to recoup its recent losses against the euro. \par \par \uc2  -- Adriana Arai in Sao Paulo contributed to this report \par \par \uc2  Write to John Hardy at john.hardy@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 2002 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020612dy6c0080x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
