\par\par \b \uc2  Euro May Benefit From Ctrl Bk Diversification Out Of Dlr \b0\par\par\uc2  By Jamie McGeever \par \uc2 606 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juni 2002\par 14:53\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  LONDON -(Dow Jones)- The euro's gains against the U.S. dollar since the end of January may be running out of steam but help from certain central banks could be at hand. \par \par \uc2  With the dollar having been under severe pressure in recent months, entering what appears to be a long-term downtrend after seven years as undisputed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  king, central banks are believed to be looking to diversify their large dollar-denominated reserves. \par \par \uc2  And it's the euro, through the purchase of euro-zone government bonds, that's likely to benefit more than any other \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts say. \par \par \uc2  "There is some reserve switching," but not much just yet, said Aziz McMahon, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN Amro. \par \par \uc2  This could really start to materialize as soon as the second quarter is over, according to UBS Warburg \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists, pointing out that central banks' asset allocations are generally done on a quarterly basis. \par \par \uc2  Michael Lewis, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Deutsche Bank, says any shift will most likely be seen some time after the summer. \par \par \uc2  "It's likely that central banks will be the next players," in the euro's rally against the dollar, Lewis said. "We are getting more discussion on the subject...but not yet (much) financial commitment." \par \par \uc2  Asian central banks, in particular, are thought to be the front runners. The central banks with the biggest reserves in the world are in Japan, China, Taiwan, Hong Kong, and South Korea. Japan, with $419.65 billion, has the largest reserves of any single country. \par \par \uc2  Indeed, having bought around $20 billion through four \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the last few weeks, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's dollar reserves are probably even higher. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par             Central Bks May Consider Dollars\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par At the end of 2000, industrialized nations' reserves were 73.3%\par denominated in dollars. For developing countries, the figure was around\par 64.3%, making the average \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's dollar reserves around 68% of\par its total, according to Deutsche Bank research.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In 1991, this was as low as 51.3%, according to Lewis, which shows the\par scope monetary authorities now have for diversifying out of the dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par And it should come as little surprise that central banks may be\par considering scaling back their dollar reserves.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Owing to a series of corporate and accounting scandals in the U.S.,\par weak U.S. stocks, fears of a profitless recovery there and a worrying\par U.S. current account deficit, dollar sentiment has soured greatly in\par recent months.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Many banks and analysts have revised down their dollar forecasts for\par this year and next and their euro forecasts upward. Whisper it, but\par parity is once again on the horizon.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par After briefly popping above $0.9500 Wednesday for the first time since\par January last year, the euro is only five cents away from that\par psychologically important level.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par And if central banks shift their multibillion dollar asset portfolios\par by only a few percent in favor of the euro, the capital flows will\par certainly be big enough to help push the euro higher still.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The gradual emergence of real money managers into the euro's upswing\par has accounted for around three cents of the move up to $0.9500 from this\par year's low of $0.8644 on Feb. 1, according to ABN Amro's McMahon.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par If central banks, traditionally considered among the most cautious of\par investors, start buying into the euro, that could reassure the rest of\par the market that the single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has more upside potential.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Jamie McGeever, Dow Jones Newswires; 44 207 842 9483;\par jamie.mcgeever@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020612dy6c000ry\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
