\par\par \b \uc2  POLL-Yen seen steady vs dollar, weak vs euro. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Ruth Pitchford \par \uc2 744 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juni 2002\par 13:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 12 (Reuters) - The yen is expected to hold steady against the dollar and weaken against the euro in coming months as the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to protect an uncertain export-led recovery, according to a Reuters poll. \par \par \uc2  The survey of 50 \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists, taken June 10-12, found growing conviction that the dollar will pursue a significant downtrend over the next year against the euro and just about every other \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  - except the yen . \par \par \uc2  The mid-range forecasts were for the yen to end June at 125.20 per dollar - near current levels - then hold around 127 over the next three to 12 months as the market accepts that the Japanese authorities intend to keep export prices competitive. \par \par \uc2  That leaves the euro to reap the gains of the dollar's fall and remain firm against the yen. The mid-range forecast is 121.50 yen per euro in 12 months, against current levels around 119 . \par \par \uc2  "The Japanese authorities have obviously \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in quite a substantial amount, making clear that they want to stabilise dollar/yen," said Hans Guenter Redeker at BNP Paribas. \par \par \uc2  "Meanwhile the market is still seeking to sell the U.S. dollar. To sell it against the yen doesn't make any sense any more simply because you have the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  working against you....but the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't have anything to say against euro appreciation." \par \par \uc2  YEN BOUNDARIES \par \par \uc2  Many strategists have been firming up their yen forecasts over the past two months as the dollar's downtrend has gathered pace and Japan's economy has shown some signs of emerging from repeated recessions. \par \par \uc2  The 12-month forecast in Reuters May \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  poll put the yen at 131 per dollar, and in April the 12-month view was 134 yen per dollar. \par \par \uc2  But most strategists agree the Bank of Japan has set some fairly clear boundaries on the yen's range, based on its own sensitivities and those of its trading partners. \par \par \uc2  "When the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  more than once, it became clear that a weaker yen does have top priority for the government," said Michael Schubert at Commerzbank in Frankfurt. \par \par \uc2  "On the other hand, there appears to be a conviction among those in power that the yen should not drop to below 135 yen to the dollar on account of other countries." \par \par \uc2  Redeker at BNP Paribas says the euro is an obvious beneficiary of Japanese policy. Like Japan, the euro zone is recovering thanks to growing world demand for its exports. \par \par \uc2  But unlike Japan, where the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is battling deflation, the ECB is worried inflation will revive and would welcome a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cut import prices. \par \par \uc2  For the ECB, "it would be better to tighten via the exchange rate rather than via higher interest rates because higher interest rates would work entirely on the domestic demand side (of the euro zone economy) where conditions are still weak," Redeker said. \par \par \uc2  He sees the euro rallying to 127.40 yen in six months before easing back to around 122 yen in 12 months. \par \par \uc2  YEN BEARS \par \par \uc2  Others are sceptical. "Quite a lot of people are in the (euro/yen) trade already," said Tony Norfield at ABN Amro. \par \par \uc2  "Then when you look at investment flows you find the equity markets being favoured aren't the euro ones...everyone's putting money into Japan, including the euro guys. So it makes you question whether there's much upside in euro/yen." \par \par \uc2  As for dollar/yen, most strategists buy the idea that the Japanese authorities will successfully curb yen strength. But not all are convinced Japan will fight to contain yen weakness. \par \par \uc2  Jane Foley at Barclays Capital forecasts the yen at 150 per dollar in 12 months as the U.S. economy recovers and the Japanese authorities let weak economic fundamentals take their toll on the yen. \par \par \uc2  "The market has been excited about the prospects of a Japanese economic recovery in recent months," Foley said. \par \par \uc2  "However, this has been export led and the yen's recent strength threatens to deter this." \par \par \uc2  Deutsche Bank, too, remains bearish, forecasting that both the dollar and the euro will buy 140 yen in 12 months time. \par \par \uc2  Deutsche analysts cite drags on the yen including negligible interest rates, weak capital spending, declining productivity and lack of reform in the debt-burdened financial sector. \par \par \uc2  FOR DETAILS OF THE POLL, DOUBLE-CLICK ON . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020612dy6c00mzw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
