\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro powers to 17-month high above $0.95. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Christina Fincher \par \uc2 501 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juni 2002\par 10:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 12 (Reuters) - The euro strode to a 17-month high against the dollar and a five-month high against the yen on Wednesday as persistent gloom on Wall Street encouraged investors to diversify out of U.S. and into euro zone assets. \par \par \uc2  The euro rose above the pyschological $0.95 for the first time since January 2001 and hit fresh five-month highs above 119 yen. \par \par \uc2  Renewed worries over corporate earnings and U.S. accounting standards sent the Dow Jones industrial average to its lowest close since November on Tuesday. U.S. stock futures were pointing to a flat to lower open, keeping investors nervous. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar is still taking a beating from U.S. equity markets and the euro's short-term uptrend remains intact," said Paul Mackel, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Dresdner Kleinwort Wasserstein. \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been successful in putting a floor under dollar/yen, so the euro is reaping most of the benefit." \par \par \uc2  Japanese authorities have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen for dollars on four days since late May, concerned that a strengthening \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could harm Japan's tentative export-led recovery. \par \par \uc2  The dollar stood at 125.50 yen in early European trade, up from six-month lows below 123 yen set at the end of May but down from Tuesday's three-week high of 125.80 yen. \par \par \uc2  BOJ STANDS PAT \par \par \uc2  The yen showed little reaction to the Bank of Japan's decision to mantain its super-loose monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  The decision was widely expected, with most economists saying Japan's recovery is still too fragile to talk of higher rates. \par \par \uc2  Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said net purchases of Japanese stocks by foreign investors rose to 931.7 billion yen ($7.44 billion) in May from April's net buying of 545.7 billion yen. \par \par \uc2  But the pace of the buying seems to have slowed significantly in June due to such negative factors as the yen's depreciation, declining Tokyo stock prices and tumbling shares on Wall Street. \par \par \uc2  "Losses on Wall Street tend to motivate global investors to cash in shareholdings elsewhere, including shares in Japan, to help offset those losses," said Shuzo Kakuta, senior deputy general manager of Mizuho Corporate Bank. \par \par \uc2  In fact, foreign investors bought only 60.5 billion yen worth of Japanese stocks in the final week of May, a steep fall from 435.9 billion in the third week of the month. \par \par \uc2  Traders said this suggested upward pressure on the yen, generated by demand for stocks, may have run out of steam. \par \par \uc2  Foreign investors bought net 894.9 billion yen worth of bonds in May, compared to net selling of 927.5 billion yen in April. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said attention was now focused on forthcoming U.S. economic data, with the Federal Reserve's Beige Book summary of economic conditions due at 1800 GMT. \par \par \uc2  U.S. retail sales and producer price data for May are due on Thursday. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020612dy6c00goq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
