\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro stands tall as U.S. stocks fall again. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Yoshiko Mori \par \uc2 979 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juni 2002\par 08:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 12 (Reuters) - The euro hit a five-month high against the yen and stayed close to its best levels against the dollar in 17-months as weaker U.S. share prices and wariness of Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  encouraged buying of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The bullish euro climbed as high as 118.98 yen in Tokyo, but faced resistance around 119 where Japanese exporters were showing strong selling interest. \par \par \uc2  As of 0550 GMT, the euro was at 118.80/93 yen against Tuesday's late U.S. level of 118.79 yen. \par \par \uc2  "Having seen the euro clearly breaking through 118 yen, the market is now targeting this year's high of 119.72 yen (hit in early January)," said Hideaki Furumaya, head of the interbank desk at Trust and Custody Services Bank. \par \par \uc2  "Stumbling U.S. share markets and a lack of confidence among global investors for corporate America is generating safe-haven buying in the euro," said Junya Tanase, global markets officer at Chase Manhattan Bank in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday, the Dow Jones industrial average sank 1.33 percent and the Nasdaq composite index 2.19 percent. \par \par \uc2  The euro was quoted at $0.9475/80 in late Asian trade compared with Tuesday's late U.S. level of $0.9481. \par \par \uc2  The euro backed off slightly against the dollar with heavy sell orders lined up to protect knockout options rumoured around $0.9500, but the market was still eager to test a 17-month high of $0.9495 touched during New York trading hours. \par \par \uc2  Knockout options give the holder the right to trade an amount of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a set price for a specified time. \par \par \uc2  But such options become worthless if the market hits a strike point, which in this case is $0.95. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was at 125.40/45 yen after edging up to an intraday high of 125.60 yen. It rose to a three-week high of 125.78 yen in New York on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar held above 125 yen before easing off in the face of heavy sell orders from exporters. \par \par \uc2  But its downside was supported thanks to the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars for yen for four days since late May. The BOJ's recent round of dollar buying was estimated to have totalled over two trillion yen ($16 billion). \par \par \uc2  BUY JAPAN \par \par \uc2  Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said net purchases of Japanese stocks by foreign investors rose to 931.7 billion yen ($7.44 billion) in May from April's net buying of 545.7 billion yen. \par \par \uc2  But the pace of the buying seems to have slowed significantly in June due to such negative factors as the yen's depreciation, declining Tokyo stock prices and tumbling shares on Wall Street. \par \par \uc2  "Losses on Wall Street tend to motivate global investors to cash in shareholdings elsewhere, including shares in Japan, to help offset those losses," said Shuzo Kakuta, senior deputy general manager of Mizuho Corporate Bank. \par \par \uc2  In fact, foreign investors bought only 60.5 billion yen worth of Japanese stocks in the final week of May, a steep fall from 435.9 billion in the third week of the month. \par \par \uc2  Traders said this suggested upward pressure on the yen, generated by demand for stocks, may have run out of steam. \par \par \uc2  Foreign investors bought net 894.9 billion yen worth of bonds in May, compared to net selling of 927.5 billion yen in April. \par \par \uc2  This was mostly in line with forecasts by fixed-income analysts who had expected foreigners to be net buyers of Japanese government bonds (JGBs) even before debt downgrading by Moody's. \par \par \uc2  Moody's cut Japan's local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  long-term debt rating by two notches to A2 from Aa3 on March 31. \par \par \uc2  "Major foreign investors had been significantly underweight in JGBs as they had been unloading them since November," said Naoki Suzuki, investment analyst at Mizuho Securities. \par \par \uc2  "I think their buying in May reflected an attempt to rebalancing their portfolio," Suzuki said. \par \par \uc2  MOODY'S IN THE SPOTLIGHT \par \par \uc2  Thomas Byrne, Moody's lead analyst for Japan, appeared in parliament on Wednesday to answer questions about the downgrade. \par \par \uc2  "Without evidence of a turnaround in the country's fundamental public debt dynamics, the rating will reflect such rising risks over time," he said. \par \par \uc2  The markets showed little reaction to the remark. \par \par \uc2  Among other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , sterling steadied with the help of a newspaper report suggesting media mogul Rupert Murdoch would campaign against Britain joining the euro zone. \par \par \uc2  Sterling has fallen five percent against the euro this year on speculation that Britain would join the European Monetary Union, along with Sweden and Denmark. \par \par \uc2  The move would have to be approved by a referendum. \par \par \uc2  Sterling held firm near the U.S. level of 64.34/64.37 pence per euro . The pound also was supported against the dollar at around $1.4726/28 . \par \par \uc2  (With additional reporting by Chikafumi Hodo). \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 0559 GMT.  All data taken from Reuters \par \par \uc2  with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at 2130 \par \par \uc2  GMT. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                        Last     US Close  % Chg