\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Again Declines On Losses in U.S. Stocks \b0\par\par\uc2  By Grainne McCarthy and John Parry \par \uc2 1,021 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juni 2002\par 00:17\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -- Once again at the mercy of weak U.S. stocks, the dollar slipped lower Tuesday against most of its major counterparts, although it managed to make considerable inroads against the yen. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's losses were most notable against sterling -- with the British \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hitting an eight-month high -- although the pound's gains were more heavily influenced by comments from News Corp. chairman Rupert Murdoch that his company's newspapers will campaign against Britain joining the euro than by aversion to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  In a relatively volatile session, the dollar had stepped higher almost across the board early in the New York day, lifted mainly by the improved tone in U.S. equities and prompting talk that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had embarked on some sort of retracement path from its recent steep losses. But stocks reversed course in early afternoon trading, as uncertainty about corporate profits and accounting standards, and all-around lack of direction continued to keep buying momentum at bay. \par \par \uc2  "You did have an initial bounce back post the Nokia announcement but we've given that back," said Marcel Kasumovich, an economist at Goldman Sachs in New York. \par \par \uc2  Nokia, the Finnish cell-phone giant, warned that its second-quarter sales won't meet its forecasts, as demand for telecommunications-network equipment continues to slide. But investors were encouraged by its assurances that it still expects to meet its earnings target for the period. \par \par \uc2  The broader equity market, however, couldn't sustain its fragile rally, which in a familiar trend pushed the dollar lower, convincing market watchers that any reprieve for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would probably be temporary in nature. \par \par \uc2  "I see weak stocks, strong bonds and a weak dollar -- those things are going to reassert themselves," said Bill Strazzullo, vice-president of securities trading and arbitrage at State Street Global Markets in Boston. \par \par \uc2  By the end of the New York session, the dollar was around its lows for the day against the euro and the Swiss franc, although it managed to hold on to some strength against the yen, thanks partly to the continued threat of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Capital flows are also helping to support the dollar against the yen, with signs that Japanese pension funds are shifting funds out of the country and foreigners are selling or taking profits on some of their Tokyo share holdings. \par \par \uc2  "The BOJ and Japanese pension funds seem to be putting a floor underneath the dollar," against the yen, said Marc Chandler, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at HSBC in New York. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. unit has clawed back about 1% against the yen during the past two sessions, reaching session highs of around YEN 125.77, above the key YEN 125.00 mark, thought to be currently acceptable to Japanese exporters and to Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  These levels are out of the so-called "discomfort zone" where the finance ministry \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  via the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  on four occasions over the past two weeks -- at levels between YEN 123.30 and YEN 123.70 in order to weaken the yen. \par \par \uc2  Late Tuesday in New York, the dollar was trading at YEN 125.23, off its session highs near YEN 125.77, but still well up from YEN 124.72 late Monday in New York. The euro was trading at 94.84 U.S. cents, up from 94.45 cents late Monday in New York. Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was at 1.5551 Swiss francs, down from 1.5595 francs late Monday in New York. Sterling was at $1.4717, up from $1.4605 late Monday in New York. \par \par \uc2  Sterling put in a strong showing, partly because of stronger economic data, but Mr. Murdoch's indication that he would like to see his stable of U.K. newspapers -- The Sun, The News of the World, the Times and The Sunday Times -- campaign against the euro in the run-up to a public referendum on the issue was also cited as a major factor in pushing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher. "This really got a lot of people's attention," said Rebecca Patterson, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at JP Morgan in New York. \par \par \uc2  The U.K. government has committed to judging whether its five economic tests for joining the European single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been met by June next year. If the tests are met, the government will schedule a referendum on the issue. \par \par \uc2  There was also some encouraging U.K. data, with manufacturing output coming in stronger than expected, rising 0.8% on the month in April, above expectations of a 0.5% rise. Industrial production, a wider measure which includes output from the energy, mining and utility sectors, rose 1.1% on the month. \par \par \uc2  The Brazilian real meanwhile fell sharply, hitting its lowest point in eight months, as worries over the government's ability to manage its hefty debt load sparked a wave of selling. With political uncertainties ahead of the October presidential elections contributing to the volatility, traders said the market is now testing the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the real. \par \par \uc2  "There is a great deal of dollar-linked debt coming due in the near-term and there is some thought the government may not roll it all over," said Mr. Chandler, at HSBC. \par \par \uc2  The real seemed to win little relief from polls showing former Health Minister Jose Serra is now clearly in second place in the presidential race, closing the gap on the leading contender Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva, of the Workers' Party, while distancing himself from his two other rivals. \par \par \uc2  Late in the New York day, the real was trading at 2.712 Brazilian reals to a dollar, compared with 2.636 reals at Monday's close. Ten days ago, the real was at 2.510 reals. \par \par \uc2  -- John Hardy contributed to this report \par \par \uc2  Write to Grainne McCarthy at grainne.mccarthy@dowjones.com and John Parry at john.parry@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 2002 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020021030dy6b006hg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
