\par\par \b \uc2  Major  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  stuck in ranges, euro still bullish. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Kazunori Takada \par \uc2 646 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Juni 2002\par 09:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 11 (Reuters) - Major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  stayed locked in tight ranges in Asian trading on Tuesday but most traders remained bullish on the euro, saying it was only a matter of time before the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regained its upward momentum. \par \par \uc2  "I think the market is cautious of position adjustments in the euro after its recent rise against the dollar," said Noriyuki Kato, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  ING in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  "There is no question that the euro is bullish so once that is cleared I think we can expect the euro to start moving towards the key level of 95.95 cents," Kato said. \par \par \uc2  The euro last traded at that level 17 months ago, in January 2001. As of 0521 GMT, the European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was at 94.45 cents, little changed from the late U.S. level of 94.42. \par \par \uc2  The euro stood at 117.65 yen, also steady compared with the late New York's level of 117.72. \par \par \uc2  The dollar shed some early gains against the yen to trade around 124.57 yen after rising to a high of 124.83 in Tokyo as traders adjusted long positions after an upside test failed due to heavy yen orders by Japanese exporters. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was trading around 124.65 in late U.S. trade. \par \par \uc2  But fears that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars and recent limited gains in Tokyo share prices were capping a further rise in the yen, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "The Nikkei stock average is having difficulties in breaking the 12,000 level so it is hard to continue buying the yen at this point," said Ryohei Muramatsu, manager of the treasury and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  division at Commerzbank in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  EURO BETTER BET THAN DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  Though some traders have said their focus is on a series of U.S. economic indicators including retail and producer price data scheduled for release later this week, many think the current bearish dollar trend is unlikely to change. \par \par \uc2  "People are moving their investments to the euro zone from the United States because of the huge U.S. current account deficit and terrorism fears," said Muramatsu at Commerzbank. \par \par \uc2  "So I think this trend will stay as it is for a while." \par \par \uc2  Reports from Washington that authorities had foiled a possible attack with a "dirty bomb" - a conventional bomb packed with radioactive material - gave nervous investors more reason to shift out of the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "It's a game of finding who has fewer negatives," said Hideki Hayashi, global strategist at Shinko Securities. \par \par \uc2  Hayashi said no major \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had decisively positive factors to lure global investors, but the euro had fewer negatives compared to the dollar and the yen. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 0524 GMT. All data taken from Reuters with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at 2130 GMT. \par \par \uc2   Last     US Close  % Chg  YTD %   2001 Cls \par \par \uc2   10 Jun. \par \par \uc2   ---------------------------------------- \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Euro/dlr       0.9446   0.9437   +0.10    +6.09   0.8904\par  Dlr/yen        124.54   124.75   -0.17    -5.39   131.63\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Euro/yen    117.66   117.72   -0.05    +0.36   117.24\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Dlr/swiss      1.5575   1.5590   -0.10