\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Climbs Against Yen as U.S. Blue Chips Close Higher \b0\par\par\uc2  By John Parry \par \uc2 813 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Juni 2002\par 00:08\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -- Rallying U.S. stocks helped burnish the bruised dollar's appeal Monday, allowing it to step higher against the yen and hold its ground against the euro. \par \par \uc2  But the greenback remains close to six-month lows against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and 16-month troughs against the euro, and analysts reckon the shift toward more dollar-positive sentiment will likely prove transitory. \par \par \uc2  Late Monday, the dollar was at 124.69 yen, well off its session highs around 124.93 yen, but still stronger than 124.41 yen late Friday in New York. The euro was at 94.41 U.S. cents, little changed from 94.40 cents late Friday. Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was at 1.5605 francs, up from 1.5576 francs late Friday in New York. Sterling was at $1.4602, up from $1.4596 late Friday in New York. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar has picked up a little bit and the primary driving force seems to be coming from the equity markets," said Ram Bhagavatula, chief economist for North America at Royal Bank of Scotland in New York. The Dow Jones Industrial Average gained 55.73 points, or 0.58%, to close at 9645.40. \par \par \uc2  Yet there was scant domestic economic data to galvanize U.S. asset markets Monday, he noted, while the moderate advance on the stock markets may not indicate any real resurgence of confidence, with many of the underlying concerns -- such as weak profits and a lack of faith in accounting standards at many U.S. corporations -- still a large focus for investors. \par \par \uc2  "There is still considerable doubt in investors' minds over the degree to which accounting issues have been fully expressed," said Michael Woolfolk, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist and head of North American research at the Bank of New York. \par \par \uc2  To be sure, there were some glimmers of hope for the dollar. For one, a temporary abatement of the tensions between India and Pakistan, after the Indian government said it sees some improvement of the tense situation on the border, is helping the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's tone improve to a degree. \par \par \uc2  Recently, "a large part of the reason for dollar weakness has been tensions" in that region, said Ed Stapleton, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Fortis USA Financial Markets in New York. That situation, "seems to be cooling down," Mr. Stapleton noted, ahead of Defense Secretary Donald H. Rumsfeld's visit to the region later this week. \par \par \uc2  That shift of market perceptions helped the dollar rise against the Swiss franc, which tends to benefit as a haven in moments of escalating geopolitical conflict. Nevertheless, the stand-off between the two countries remains a potentially volatile and unpredictable situation. \par \par \uc2  Some softer European data also gave the dollar some back-handed support, helping take the wind out of the euro's sails and preventing the single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from staging an assault on the 95-cent mark, which it last traded above in January 2001. In Europe's biggest economy, German monthly retail sales fell 2.4%, clearly weaker than expected. \par \par \uc2  Yet on this side of the Atlantic, even robust U.S. data hasn't given the dollar much of a lift lately, revealing the extent of prevailing negative sentiment towards the greenback. During recent weeks, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  "has been selling off on bad information [among U.S. economic data reports] and standing pat on good information," said Mr. Woolfolk, who expects to see that trend continue. \par \par \uc2  Among the next major tests for the greenback's performance is the publication of the Federal Reserve's Beige Book report on Wednesday. This summary of economic activity in different U.S. regions will be closely watched for hints as to when the U.S. economic rebound might warrant an interest-rate increase, which could bolster the dollar's performance. \par \par \uc2  Should stocks and the dollar relapse on disappointment over U.S. data, the prospects of more Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  aimed at weakening the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would loom once again. In the current dollar environment, traders and analysts are very conscious that the BOJ, acting on behalf of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars for yen in at least four rounds during the past few weeks at levels between 123.30 yen and 123.70 yen. \par \par \uc2  Some market participants speculate that a Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  meeting later this week will keep the BOJ from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  again for now, but Tim Mazanec, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Investors Bank & Trust in Boston said that he expects the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to continue watching the yen's performance very closely and to step in again to buy dollars at around 123.60 yen if the dollar should retreat to those levels. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 2002 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020610dy6a006bt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
