\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar turns tail vs euro, eyes 17-month lows. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sumeet Desai \par \uc2 471 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Juni 2002\par 12:34\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 10 (Reuters) - The dollar erased early gains against the euro on Monday to eye 17-month lows hit last week as investors continued to doubt the pace of the U.S. economic recovery and the appeal of U.S. assets. \par \par \uc2  The dollar earlier fought back from last week's lows as a late recovery in U.S. stock markets on Friday and the failure of the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to pierce key technical resistance at $0.95 encouraged some investors to cut their euro positions. \par \par \uc2  But dealers said that the euro's dip to below $0.94 only provided a better buying opportunity for the dollar bears and the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  climbed to $0.9453 by 1024 GMT. \par \par \uc2  "It seems the dollar is heading down," said Russell Jones, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Lehman Brothers. \par \par \uc2  "Patchy economic performance, poor stock markets and low interest rates. That combination will help keep the dollar under pressure." \par \par \uc2  But the greenback was firmer against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , trading around two yen up from recent six-month lows, as traders remained wary that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen again, as it has done on four days in the last few weeks. \par \par \uc2  Euro sentiment was hurt early on by news that German retail sales fell in April even though the month had two more business days than the same month last year. Sales in the euro zone's biggest economy have been down in annual terms every month since December. \par \par \uc2  FIRM FLOOR SET FOR DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  The dollar rose against the yen as \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's repeated yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the past few weeks proved effective in setting a firm floor for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The greenback was up a quarter percent at 124.65 yen, drawing support from comments by senior \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official Zembei Mizoguchi who said on Monday that Japan was ready to take action in the market when deemed necessary. \par \par \uc2  "Dollar/yen's been helped by Mizoguchi. The market is very aware that the Bank of Japan will respond if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves become too rapid," said Kamal Sharma, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Commerzbank. \par \par \uc2  Japanese authorities have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the yen's rapid rise on four days in the past few weeks after the dollar fell below 124.00 yen. \par \par \uc2  Traders estimate the Bank of Japan spent well over two trillion yen in the four days that it entered the market. \par \par \uc2  Still, while Japanese authorities have for now artificially kept the yen's rise in check, analysts said that in the longer term the dollar would continue to come under pressure. \par \par \uc2  The euro was slightly higher on the day at 117.75 yen but still shy of four-month highs just below 118 hit on Friday. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020610dy6a00ls7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
