\par\par \b \uc2  Euro backs off vs dlr, yen but undertone bullish. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Chikafumi Hodo \par \uc2 845 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Juni 2002\par 08:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 10 (Reuters) - The euro backed off against the dollar and yen in Tokyo on Monday as dealers unwound heavy long positions after its failure to breach a key resistance point, but its undertone stayed bullish. \par \par \uc2  The single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  edged to an intraday low of $0.9389 on stop-loss selling after having hit a fresh 17-month high of $0.9490 on Friday, failing to reach $0.95 as U.S. stocks shaved their losses, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  As of 0557 GMT, the euro was finding support around $0.94 as the market was careful about selling it too actively amid caution about how U.S. shares will perform later in the day, they said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the euro may test its upside against the dollar again once the market completes profit-taking, partly on expectations that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will raise interest rates sooner than U.S. Federal Reserve. \par \par \uc2  "There are still heavy euro long positions against the dollar and the yen. After seeing the euro's early losses, I thought it would fall further, but its drop was pretty much limited," a senior trader at a Japanese trust bank said. \par \par \uc2  "It seems that the market is still very bullish on the euro, but apparently there are major selling orders to protect knockout options at $0.95," he said. \par \par \uc2  The market continued to keep a close eye on weak and volatile U.S. stocks as these reflect deepening concern among global investors about the sustainability of the U.S. current account deficit and corporate profits, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Last week, major market gauges racked up their worst weekly performances since late April. The Dow Jones industrial average slumped 3.4 percent, the Nasdaq composite index lost five percent and the Standard & Poor's 500 index lost 3.7 percent. \par \par \uc2  At one point on Friday the Dow sank to 9,472.54, but managed to end down just 0.36 percent at 9,589.67. \par \par \uc2  In late Tokyo trade on Monday the euro stood at 117.42/52 yen with exporters lining up heavy sell orders around 118 yen. On Friday, it had hit a four-month high of 117.98 yen. \par \par \uc2  FIRM FLOOR SET FOR DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  The dollar rose against the yen as \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's repeated yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the past few weeks has proved effective in setting a firm floor for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But the greenback was caught in tight ranges by late afternoon, with selling interest strong around 124.80-124.90 yen, while bids were lined up around 124.50-124.60 yen, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar stood at 124.79/85 yen against Friday's U.S. level of 124.37/45. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar managed to stay above 124 yen, even when it was being sold overnight," said Shogo Nagaya, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Nomura Trust and Banking. \par \par \uc2  "Slowly, the floor for the dollar-yen is being raised. The \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  seem to have had their effect." \par \par \uc2  Senior Japanese Finance Ministry official Zembei Mizoguchi said on Monday that the ministry remained ready to take action in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market when deemed necessary. \par \par \uc2  "As we have been saying, excessive and rapid movements are not appropriate and we have been sending that signal. That situation remains unchanged," Mizoguchi, head of the ministry's international bureau, told reporters. \par \par \uc2  Japanese authorities has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the yen's rapid rise on four days in the past few weeks after the dollar fell below 124.00 yen. \par \par \uc2  Traders estimate the Bank of Japan spent well over two trillion yen in the four days that it entered the market. \par \par \uc2  Still, while Japanese authorities have for now artificially kept the yen's rise in check, analysts said that in the longer term the dollar would continue to come under pressure. \par \par \uc2  "It's only the fear of getting the hand slapped that keeps them (investors) away from the cookie jar," said Marshall Gittler, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of America in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  "But gradually, they'll keep testing and over time, move the MOF's accepted range gradually lower." \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 0607 GMT. All data taken from Reuters \par \par \uc2  with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at 2130 \par \par \uc2  GMT. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                        Last     US Close  % Chg  YTD %   2001 Cls\par                                 07 Jun.\par                        -----------------------------------------\par  Euro/dlr              0.9414   0.9433   -0.20    -0.86   0.9496\par  Dlr/yen               124.76   124.33   +0.35    +9.01   114.45\par  Euro/yen              117.45   117.32   +0.11    +8.04   108.71\par  Dlr/swiss             1.5614   1.5582   +0.21    -2.49   1.6012\par  Stg/dlr               1.4564   1.4594   -0.21    -2.91   1.5000\par  Can/dlr               1.5336   1.5318   +0.12    +2.83   1.4914\par  Aus/dlr               0.5705   0.5712   -0.12    +1.98   0.5594\par  Euro/swiss            1.4700   1.4700   +0.00    -3.42   1.5221\par  Euro/stg              0.6458   0.6462   -0.06    +2.10   0.6325.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020610dy6a00dah\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscal