\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar gains vs yen - BOJ provides firm floor. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Kazunori Takada \par \uc2 452 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Juni 2002\par 04:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 10 (Reuters) - The dollar posted modest gains against the yen on Monday morning with traders saying \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's repeated yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the past few weeks had proved effective in sealing a firm floor for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The dollar managed to stay above 124 yen, even when it was being sold overnight," said Shogo Nagaya, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Nomura Trust and Banking. \par \par \uc2  "Slowly, the floor for the dollar-yen is being raised. The \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  seem to have had their effect." \par \par \uc2  As of 0237 GMT, the dollar stood at 124.73 yen against Friday's late U.S. level of 124.37. \par \par \uc2  Traders estimate the Bank of Japan spent well over two trillion yen ($16 billion) in the four days that it entered the market over the past few weeks when the dollar slipped below 124 yen. \par \par \uc2  "I think a good deal of (yen) demand was absorbed through the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," a Japanese city bank trader said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also made some headway against the euro on position adjustment. \par \par \uc2  "Many were long on the euro so they may be cutting back such positions. But the move is purely technical and expected to have little effect in the long run," another Japanese city back dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the dollar remains under pressure against the euro, with some saying they even expected the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to jump as high as 96.00 cents during the week, partly on expectations the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will raise interest rates sooner than the U.S. Federal Reserve. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at 94.03 cents against 94.33 in late New York. Against the yen, it was flat at 117.31 yen \par \par \uc2  "Euro/yen is strong but not enough to set a trend. It is mostly following movements in the dollar/yen or euro/dollar," Nomura Trust's Nagaya said. \par \par \uc2  BOJ EFFECT DOUBTFUL LONG-TERM \par \par \uc2  Still, while Japanese authorities have for now artificially kept the yen's rise in check, analysts said that in the longer term, the dollar would continue to come under pressure. \par \par \uc2  "It's only the fear of getting the hand slapped that keeps them (investors) away from the cookie jar," said Marshall Gittler, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of America in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  "But gradually, they'll keep testing and over time, move the MOF's accepted range gradually lower." \par \par \uc2  The market showed no reaction to news that Japan's domestic wholesale price index was unchanged in May from the previous month. The May index fell 1.2 percent from a year earlier for the 20th straight month of year-on-year decline. ($1=124.45 Yen). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020610dy6a0060v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
