\par\par \b \uc2  WRAP:Japan Ends Recession With 1Q GDP Jump; Outlook Weak \b0\par\par\uc2  By Audrey McAvoy \par \uc2 995 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Juni 2002\par 07:21\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- Japan ended its deepest postwar recession in the first quarter as rebounding exports and solid consumer spending spurred the economy to impressive growth. \par \par \uc2  Gross domestic product jumped a real 1.4% in the first three months of the year from the previous quarter, or an annual rate of 5.7%. That growth, the first in a year and the fastest in two years, matched the consensus estimate of 25 economists surveyed by Dow Jones Newswires. \par \par \uc2  But the rebound is precariously dependent on overseas demand and could be endangered by the yen's recent rise. The government said the GDP data confirm the worst is over, but officials vowed to press on with measures to bolster the economy. \par \par \uc2  "We must consider how to extend this into an economic recovery," Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi told reporters. "We will proceed with economic steps." \par \par \uc2  Koizumi is compiling a tax reform and deregulation package, billed as ensuring recovery and setting the stage for long-term growth, which he will present to his fellow leaders of the Group of Eight leading nations at a summit in three weeks. \par \par \uc2  But for the moment, the first quarter GDP growth underscored the boost Japan is getting from the recovering U.S. and Asian economies. The rising demand for exports has fueled a pickup in industrial output, triggering a mild cyclical recovery. \par \par \uc2  But even with the upturn, Japan's GDP shrank 1.3% for the fiscal year through March, the steepest decline since current calculation methods were adopted in 1980 and a tad worse than the government's official forecast of a 1.0% decline. \par \par \uc2  The economy shriveled for three straight quarters through December as the information technology boom went bust, sending Japanese exports and production plummeting. Manufacturers slashed investment and jobs in response, chilling consumer sentiment and further damping already sluggish domestic demand. \par \par \uc2  The current upturn is widely expected to be tepid, as the government expects GDP to be flat this fiscal year. \par \par \uc2  Japan's first quarter economic rebound is more spindly than it looks, many economists said, because the personal spending figure was likely overstated, companies continue to slash capital spending and government spending is on the wane. \par \par \uc2  Defying notions that Japanese consumers are reluctant to open their wallets as wages decline and job insecurity mounts, consumption as measured by GDP rose 1.6% in January-March from the previous quarter. \par \par \uc2  But many economists question the reliability of the data on consumption - which accounts for more than half of GDP. \par \par \uc2  Much of the sample data comes from single-person households, who tend to log stronger consumption than families, and seasonal adjustments not done smoothly, said Daiwa SMBC chief market economist Seiji Shiraishi. \par \par \uc2  "The actual (GDP growth) should be weaker than the data showed," Shiraishi said. \par \par \uc2  Economy Minister Heizo Takenaka said consumption data fluctuate greatly and acknowledged a gap between actual consumer behavior and data results. \par \par \uc2  "We need to assess the numbers with a degree of caution," Takenaka said. \par \par \uc2  And even if consumption is relatively firm for the time being, economists said, it is likely to weaken later in the year as companies continue to restructure and cut wages, depressing disposable incomes. \par \par \uc2  Moreover, business investment in factories and equipment, the second-biggest chunk of GDP, continued to slide in the first quarter. Capital spending sank 3.2% as companies, still stinging from a sharp fall in profits last year and swimming in excess capacity, slashed investment. That was on top of a 12.0% fall the previous quarter. \par \par \uc2  Even the vibrant export growth in the first quarter may not be sustainable. Exports jumped 6.4%, the first rise in a year and a half. External demand boosted GDP growth 0.7% for the quarter - the biggest contribution on record. \par \par \uc2  But that's been endangered by the recent rise in the yen's exchange rate, which makes Japan's exports more expensive in other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It also hurts the economy by crimping the yen value of Japanese companies' overseas earnings and magnifying Japan's persistent deflation. \par \par \uc2  As the dollar has slumped to six-month lows, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market four times in recent weeks, selling yen to brake the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's ascent. \par \par \uc2  Aside from trying to cheapen their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , policymakers have few tools left to sustain the economy. \par \par \uc2  Government debt has swelled to more than 130% of GDP after a decade of government spending packages and recession-choked tax revenues, sharply constraining fiscal policy. \par \par \uc2  And with interest rates at virtually zero, there's little the Bank of Japan can do. The BOJ now floods the Tokyo money market with more cash than banks can use in a "quantitative easing" meant to fight deflation and bolster the bad loan-laden financial system. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is committed to maintaining this "quantitative easing" until prices stop falling, likely won't be able to change policy for the foreseeable future. The January-March GDP deflator, a measure of prices, remained negative for a 16th straight quarter. \par \par \uc2  And the government continues to push for more support from the BOJ. \par \par \uc2  "It's only natural that we say we want more liquidity," said Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa. "It would be stupid of us to ask them to tighten monetary policy now." \par \par \uc2  Tokyo markets reacted little to the GDP data as it matched expectations. \par \par \uc2  The dollar traded at Y124.30 around 0745 GMT, little changed from Y124.22 late Thursday in New York. Tokyo shares dropped 1.2% on the benchmark Nikkei 225 Stock Average, but traders attributed the fall to an overnight Wall Street slide, not Japan's GDP. \par \par \uc2  Japanese government bond prices rose, pushing the 10-year yield down 1.5 basis points to 1.36%. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By Audrey McAvoy, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929;\par audrey.mcavoy@dowjones.com\par    (James Simms, Miyako Takebe, Asako Tanabe, Jonathan Soble and\par Michiyo Seki contributed to this report.)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020607dy670008y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
