\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-Japan official reserves hit record high in May. \b0\par\par\uc2 331 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Juni 2002\par 02:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 7 (Reuters) - Japan's official reserves rose to a record high in May after dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to cap the yen's rise, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Japan's foreign reserves - a key gauge of the country's ability to meet its foreign obligations - were at $419.652 billion at the end of May, up from $406.700 billion in April, MOF said. \par \par \uc2  "The main reasons for the increase are...actions we took on the 22nd and 23rd (in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market)...and a 3.7 percent rise month-on-month in the euro," a MOF official said. \par \par \uc2  "(Our official reserves) still remain the biggest in world." \par \par \uc2  The BOJ, acting as an agent of MOF, is estimated to have bought $8.56 billion in the two bouts of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to banking sources. It \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  again on May 31 but those trades were settled in June. \par \par \uc2  "We spent a good amount of money for the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  but for details please look at figures that will be announced in early August," the official said, declining further comment. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also stepped in to buy dollars for yen on Tuesday during early New York trading hours. \par \par \uc2  The euro rose to 93.71 cents by the end of May from 90.02 cents at the end of April, according to the ministry. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese authorities, fearing that a surge in the yen would hurt Japan's fragile, export-led recovery, have repeatedly warned they would step into the market whenever moves were deemed excessive. \par \par \uc2  Traders reckoned the BOJ had spent well over two trillion yen in all four \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's foreign reserves have long been the biggest in the world, outstripping those of the 12-nation euro zone and the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  which stood at a combined $252.9 billion at the end of March. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020607dy67000sd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
