\par\par \b \uc2  Weak Stocks, Global Tension Put the Dollar Under Pressure \b0\par\par\uc2  By John Parry \par \uc2 639 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Juni 2002\par 00:29\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK --  Tumbling U.S. stock markets Thursday all but snuffed out the sense of optimism the dollar had enjoyed earlier in the week, leaving the greenback lower against the euro and the yen late in the New York session. \par \par \uc2  Although off its nadir earlier in the global day, the dollar plumbed fresh sixteen-month lows against the euro during the New York morning and continued to languish at six-month lows against the yen. \par \par \uc2  Against the majors late Thursday, the dollar was at 124.22 yen, having traded in a fairly wide range, well off its session lows, but down from 124.42 yen late Wednesday in New York. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at 94.53 U.S. cents, off its session highs of around 94.71 cents but having made a strong advance from 93.96 cents late Wednesday in New York. \par \par \uc2  Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was at 1.5579 Swiss francs down from 1.5648 francs late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Sterling was at $1.4592, little changed from $1.4590 late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar is still under pressure," said John McCarthy, director of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at ING Barings Capital Markets in New York. "Just when you think that the dollar has bottomed out for the time being, then that's not the case," he said, citing "continued concern about the value of equities" as the key catalyst for the dollar's relapse. \par \par \uc2  By Thursday's closing, the Dow Jones Industrial Average had dropped 172 points to about 9625. \par \par \uc2  As a result, the euro broke above some key levels, capitalizing on the dollar's despondent tone, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders said. \par \par \uc2  The decisions of both the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Bank of England to leave key interest rates unchanged Thursday took the sheen off their respective \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' performances against the dollar at first. But later in the global session the euro was benefiting from its digestion of stronger-than-expected data from Europe's biggest economy. \par \par \uc2  German manufacturing orders rose in April for the first time this year, and much more strongly than analysts had estimated, offering clearer evidence of a long-awaited economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  The dollar sustained relatively shallow losses against the yen, however, because of continued fears that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  would again \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , via the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , so as to weaken the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  More jawboning from top Japanese officials helped keep that prospect looming top of mind and shored up the dollar's performance against the yen. Early Thursday, Haruhiko Kuroda, vice finance minister for international affairs, said the yen had risen too far and reiterated Tokyo's resolve to prevent the strong yen from derailing a nascent economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  "The excessive yen appreciation is undesirable," Mr. Kuroda said, adding that there is "no change in our \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  stance." \par \par \uc2  Other factors that may weigh on the dollar's performance against widely traded rivals include persistent worries about the growing U.S. current account deficit and the chance of sporadic flight-to-quality bids on any escalation of tensions between India and Pakistan or a flare-up in the Middle East, as occurred late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  A more immediate concern is Friday's monthly U.S. employment report, a key indication of just how effectively the U.S. economic recovery is taking hold. \par \par \uc2  Among other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the focus of Latin American markets once again was on the performance of the Brazilian real, which earlier in the week charted its biggest one-day decline against the dollar since September. Late Thursday in New York, the real was at 2.6610 to the dollar, compared with 2.6085 late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Write to John Parry at john.parry@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 2002 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020606dy660080x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
