\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Weak US stocks, rate differentials sink dollar. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Daniel Bases \par \uc2 949 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Juni 2002\par 23:42\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, June 6 (Reuters) - The euro hit a 16-month high versus the dollar on Thursday as prospects for a widening interest rate differential between Europe and the United States and a big drop in U.S. stocks pulled the dollar broadly lower. \par \par \uc2  The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as expected, left interest rates unchanged at 3.25 percent on Thursday, but with inflation pressures mounting, investors are betting officials will move rates higher sooner rather than later. \par \par \uc2  Analysts estimate the ECB might move before the U.S. Federal Reserve hikes its benchmark interest rate, currently at a 40-year low of 1.75 percent. The Fed is expected to stay on hold through the summer months and perhaps longer. \par \par \uc2  "The market is back to looking at interest rate differentials. While stocks remain important, the fact that we are going to get an ECB rate hike well before the Fed raises rates makes the euro that much more attractive," said Lara Rhame, U.S. economist at Brown Brothers Harriman in New York. \par \par \uc2  In his assessment of inflation risks, ECB President Wim Duisenberg said: "The risks have moved in an upward direction. But we need more information, more data, to come to our ultimate judgment." \par \par \uc2  Duisenberg said the euro's appreciation would help contain inflation expectations and renewed a call for reforms by euro zone governments that would help boost noninflationary growth. \par \par \uc2  A steep drop in U.S. stock prices also contributed to investor flight from the dollar. Semiconductor giant, Intel Corp. was downgraded by Merrill Lynch, along with a host of other stocks in the sector, casting a pall over equities and in turn the dollar. \par \par \uc2  In New York trade, the euro bought 94.72 U.S. cents, up 0.88 percent compared with Wednesday's New York close, and just off a late surge to a fresh 16-month high of 94.78 cents. \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell to 124.06 yen, a loss of 0.29 percent on the day, but roughly 1-1/2 yen above a 6 month low reached last week. \par \par \uc2  On Wall Street, U.S. stocks fell sharply, with the Dow Jones industrial average sliding 1.76 percent; the Nasdaq Composite index dropping 2.53 percent; and the broader market S&P 500 stock index losing 1.98 percent. \par \par \uc2  EMPLOYMENT IS THE KEY \par \par \uc2  The euro also got a boost from a published report that unemployment in Germany - the euro zone's largest economy - fell in May by a non-seasonally adjusted 77,600. \par \par \uc2  Traders have favored Europe's single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the dollar despite the broadening view that the U.S. economy is recovering from last year's shallow recession and outpacing the euro zone's economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  On Friday, the release of May U.S. employment data - expected to show a gain of 58,000 in non-farm jobs as well as a rise in the unemployment rate to 6.1 percent - will help investors assess whether the recent string of strong U.S. economic data is something to bank on. \par \par \uc2  "We still have a disconnect between economic fundamentals and market performance," said Alex Beuzelin, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Ruesch International. "In the end, the market's sentiment is still bearish on U.S. assets and the dollar, and that's why the euro is moving back to its 16-month highs." \par \par \uc2  The dollar was briefly boosted by news that jobless claims in the latest week fell to 383,000, the lowest in over a year. \par \par \uc2  "The initial claims number was a real shot in the arm for the dollar," said Tom Benfer, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  director at Bank of Montreal in New York. "Even when there is good news, the market tends to look the other way. Psychology has been so damaged that it's more inclined to sell the dollar." \par \par \uc2  DON'T FIGHT \b \highlight22\uc2 JAPAN\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  On Tuesday, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the fourth time in two weeks to stem the yen's strength, which undercuts exports at a time when the world's second-largest economy is emerging from a deep recession. Exports are seen as the country's sole means of growth. \par \par \uc2  Market participants are closely watching Japan's first-quarter economic growth data, due on Friday. A Reuters consensus poll forecasts that, on average, the economy grew by 6 percent. \par \par \uc2  Economists, who had forecast Japan's economy had grown as much as 10 percent in the period, are rushing to revise their forecasts downward following data this week showing weaker-than-expected capital spending by Japanese companies. \par \par \uc2  However, even if the GDP data shows a robust rebound, dealers expect any fresh yen buying to be countered by Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 2102GMT.  All data taken from Reuters \par \par \uc2  calculated from the levels at 4:30 p.m. (2030 GMT) in the \par \par \uc2  previous New York session. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                      Last     US Close   %     YTD %   2001\par                               05 Jun.  Change  Change  Close\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -------------------------------------------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Euro/dlr             0.9472   0.9389   +0.88   +7.22   0.8834\par Dlr/yen              124.06   124.42   -0.29   -5.36   131.08\par Euro/yen             117.55   116.84   +0.61   +1.55   115.76\par Dlr/swiss            1.5555  