\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro takes ECB in stride, dollar eyes Wall St. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Charlotte Cooper \par \uc2 787 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Juni 2002\par 14:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 6 (Reuters) - The euro held steady close to the day's highs against the dollar after the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  left interest rates unchanged as expected on Thursday, while the beleaguered dollar held on to session gains against the yen. \par \par \uc2  The ECB left its key refinancing rate steady at 3.25 percent following its council meeting, helping the euro to hold close to the day's highs against the yen and the Swiss franc. "Everyone expected no change," said Jeremy Hawkins, chief economic adviser at Bank of America. \par \par \uc2  "The focus will be on the news conference later to see if it was a close decision or whether they are all comfortable with rates as they stand at the moment." \par \par \uc2  ECB President Wim Duisenberg holds a news conference at 1230 GMT to explain the ECB council's interest rate decision. \par \par \uc2  By 1211 GMT the euro had pared earlier losses against the greenback to stand unchanged on the day and just over half a cent below this week's 16-month high of $0.9453. \par \par \uc2  It was also nearly a quarter percent up on the yen and the Swiss franc and a fraction firmer against the British pound. \par \par \uc2  The dollar held on to a quarter percent gain to 124.75 yen, with traders wary of trying to push it lower after Japanese authorities on Tuesday \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen for the fourth day in two weeks. \par \par \uc2  The market, which has seen the dollar's fortunes tied to those of U.S. equity markets recently, was now looking ahead to Wall Street's opening, where U.S. stock futures indicated a mixed start. \par \par \uc2  EUROPE ON HOLD \par \par \uc2  The ECB was not the only European \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hold off tightening policy on Thursday. The Bank of England and Sweden's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  both left rates unchanged as expected and neither the pound nor the crown reacted. \par \par \uc2  The euro began to pick up against the dollar after a surprise surge in German manufacturing orders in April. Data showed a rise in domestic orders and raised hopes that the euro zone's largest economy may finally have turned the corner. \par \par \uc2  Hawkins noted however that questions still remained over whether France would meet budget deficit reduction goals set out with European Union partners. France's finance minister said the country would try to meet a 2004 budget balance goal but that this year's deficit would be greater than previously forecast. \par \par \uc2  "There has been some good news and some bad news out for the euro today but by and large it's still the case that people are focusing on the U.S.," he said. \par \par \uc2  After Wall Street gains on Wednesday ended a bout of steep losses, a fall in shares of U.S. chip giant Intel after a reported analyst's downgrade was expected to weigh on U.S. stocks at the start. \par \par \uc2  Concerns about the U.S. recovery and corporate accounting practices there have upset the dollar in recent sessions. \par \par \uc2  Richmond Federal Reserve President Alfred Broaddus said on Wednesday the U.S recovery was firmly in place but how strongly and rapidly it proceeded from here was an open question. \par \par \uc2  A stronger-than-expected reading on Wednesday for the U.S. services sector Institute of Supply Management index for May offered only marginal relief. \par \par \uc2  "Yes the economy is picking up but is it translating into profits and a better tone in the equity markets? That's the concern," said Rob Hayward, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN Amro. \par \par \uc2  "Until we see a bounce in the equity markets then it's unlikely we'll see anything but a negative tone for the dollar." \par \par \uc2  In the short term, U.S. jobs data due on Friday is seen as critical for the dollar. The employment report is expected to show the economy added a modest 58,000 jobs in May, following a 43,000 gain in April. \par \par \uc2  TUG OF WAR \par \par \uc2  Investors were also focused on Japan's key January-March gross domestic product data on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Economists, who had forecast Japan's economy had grown as much as 10 percent in the period, are rushing to revise down their forecasts following data this week showing weaker than expected capital spending by Japanese companies. \par \par \uc2  However, even if the GDP data shows a robust rebound, dealers expect any fresh yen buying to be countered by Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The BOJ has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on four days in the past couple of weeks - each time when the dollar was below 124 yen. Dealers estimate it has sold over 2.0 trillion yen ($16.11 billion) in the past few weeks to weaken the yen. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020606dy6600o8k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
