\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar gets mild respite amid ECB, US stocks vigil. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Charlotte Cooper \par \uc2 816 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Juni 2002\par 12:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 6 (Reuters) - The dollar won a mild reprieve against the euro and the yen on Thursday after its recent rout to 2002 lows, as uncertainty on the outlook for U.S. stocks and a European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  interest rate meeting dampened activity. \par \par \uc2  Traders were wary of trying to push the dollar further down against the yen given Japan's repeated attempts in the past two weeks to curb export-damaging yen strength. \par \par \uc2  "There's not much to watch for apart from the interest rate meetings. People are reasonably confident that the ECB are not going to do anything but are cautious just in case," said Rob Hayward, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN Amro. \par \par \uc2  "Primarily people are keeping their eyes on the performance of U.S. equities this afternoon but everyone is still very cautious." \par \par \uc2  The dollar lost some of the session's gains against the euro after a surprise surge in German manufacturing orders in April. Data showed a rise in domestic orders and raised hopes that the euro zone's largest economy may finally have turned the corner. \par \par \uc2  But by 1024 GMT the dollar was still in positive territory for the day and just over half a cent above this week's 16-month low of $0.9453 per euro. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen the dollar gained a third of a percent on the day to 124.85 yen, two yen above last week's six-month low. \par \par \uc2  The ECB announces its rate decision at 1145 GMT and holds a news conference with ECB President Wim Duisenberg at 1230 GMT. \par \par \uc2  A Reuters poll carried out last week showed 49 of 52 economists expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the key euro zone rate unchanged at 3.25 percent. EUROPE ON HOLD Analysts said they doubted an interest rate hike would benefit the euro at this stage when recovery is still soft. \par \par \uc2  "If the ECB hikes rates today, it would be negative for the euro because a rate hike is not 100 percent necessary when inflationary pressure is abating," said Francesca Fornasari, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Lehman Brothers. \par \par \uc2  Earlier, the Swedish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  left its interest rates on hold as expected after two rate rises this year and the Bank of England is also due to announce its rate decision at 1100 GMT, with most analysts expecting no change. \par \par \uc2  With not much to move markets in the European morning, traders and analysts were looking ahead to the opening on Wall Street, after gains on Wednesday ended a bout of steep losses. \par \par \uc2  Concerns about the U.S. recovery and corporate accounting practices there have upset the dollar in recent sessions and U.S. stock futures were pointing to a mixed start. \par \par \uc2  Richmond Federal Reserve President Alfred Broaddus said on Wednesday the U.S recovery was firmly in place but how strongly and rapidly it proceeded from here was an open question. \par \par \uc2  A stronger-than-expected reading on Wednesday for the U.S. services sector Institute of Supply Management index for May offered only marginal relief. \par \par \uc2  "Yes the economy is picking up but is it translating into profits and a better tone in the equity markets? That's the concern," Hayward said. \par \par \uc2  "Until we see a bounce in the equity markets then it's unlikely we'll see anything but a negative tone for the dollar." \par \par \uc2  The dollar's medium-term picture is still clouded by global capital flows out of the United States. Data released by Japan's Finance Ministry showed that foreign investors were net buyers of Japanese stocks for the third consecutive month in May. \par \par \uc2  Foreign investors have been underweight in Japanese equities but experts say new optimism towards the world's second biggest economy as well as lingering pessimism over the U.S. stock market gives them a strong incentive to pour money into Japan. \par \par \uc2  In the short term, U.S. jobs data due on Friday is seen as critical for the dollar. The employment report is expected to show the economy added a modest 58,000 jobs in May, following a 43,000 gain in April. \par \par \uc2  TUG OF WAR \par \par \uc2  Investors were also focused on Japan's key January-March gross domestic product data on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Economists, who had forecast Japan's economy had grown as much as 10 percent in the period, are rushing to revise down their forecasts following data this week showing weaker than expected capital spending by Japanese companies. \par \par \uc2  However, even if the GDP data shows a robust rebound, dealers expect any fresh yen buying to be countered by Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The BOJ has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on four days in the past couple of weeks - each time when the dollar was below 124 yen. Dealers estimate it has sold over 2.0 trillion yen ($16.11 billion) in the past few weeks to weaken the yen. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020606dy6600ku3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
