\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar wins reprieve, markets on ECB rate vigil. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Natsuko Waki \par \uc2 660 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Juni 2002\par 10:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 6 (Reuters) - The dollar won a reprieve against the yen and the euro on Thursday after its recent rout to 2002 lows, with investors keeping a vigil on key rate-setting meetings by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Bank of England. \par \par \uc2  Traders were also cautious in pushing the dollar further down against the yen given Japan's repeated attempts in the past few weeks to curb export-damaging yen strength. \par \par \uc2  "It's a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  day and the focus is on Europe. Recent U.S. data have been somewhat positive and it allowed the dollar to pause a bit," said Francesca Fornasari, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Lehman Brothers. \par \par \uc2  "If the ECB hikes rates today, it would be negative for the euro because a rate hike is not 100 percent necessary when inflationary pressure is abating." \par \par \uc2  A Reuters poll carried out last week showed 49 of 52 economists expect the ECB to keep key rates unchanged at 3.25 percent. The ECB is to announce its rate decision at 1145 GMT and to hold news conference at 1230 GMT. \par \par \uc2  The greenback bounced nearly half a percent against the euro on the day to $0.9350 by 0803 GMT, up a cent from its six-month low of $0.9453 set on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen the dollar also gained ground, standing at 124.91 compared with 124.42 in late New York trading on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The euro was steady against the yen at 116.80 . \par \par \uc2  The BoE also announces its rate decision at 1100 GMT, with most analysts expecting no change. \par \par \uc2  PERSISTENT WORRIES \par \par \uc2  Concerns about the U.S. recovery and corporate accounting practices there have upset the dollar in recent sessions. Wednesday's surge on Wall Street and a stronger than expected reading for the U.S. services sector Institute of Supply Management index for May offered only marginal relief. \par \par \uc2  U.S. stocks futures are pointing to a lower opening on Wall street later in the day. \par \par \uc2  Richmond Federal Reserve President Alfred Broaddus said on Wednesday the U.S recovery was firmly in place but how strongly and rapidly it proceeded from here was an open question. \par \par \uc2  With this in mind, the dollar's medium-term picture is still clouded by global capital flows out of the United States, favouring Japan and other assets. \par \par \uc2  Data released by Japan's Finance Ministry showed that foreign investors were net buyers of Japanese stocks for the third consecutive month in May. \par \par \uc2  Foreign investors had long been underweight in Japanese equities, but experts say new optimism towards the world's second biggest economy as well as lingering pessimism over the U.S. stock market gave them a strong incentive to pour money into Japan. \par \par \uc2  "The near-term outlook for the Japanese stock market is much better than that for the United States," said Takashi Yamanaka, an economist at UFJ Bank in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  In the short term, U.S. jobs data, due on Friday, is seen as critical for the dollar. The employment report is expected to show the economy added a modest 58,000 jobs in May, following a 43,000 gain in April. \par \par \uc2  TUG OF WAR \par \par \uc2  Investors were also focused on Japan's key January-March gross domestic product data on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Economists, who had forecast Japan's economy had grown as much as 10 percent in the period, are rushing to revise down their forecasts following data this week showing weaker than expected capital spending by Japanese companies. \par \par \uc2  However, even if the GDP data shows a robust rebound, dealers expect any fresh yen buying to be countered by Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The BOJ has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on four days in the past couple of weeks - each time when the dollar was below 124 yen. Dealers estimate it has sold over 2.0 trillion yen ($16.11 billion) in the past few weeks to curb export-damaging yen strength. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020606dy6600gz6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
