\par\par \b \uc2  Yen Defies Gravity Of Japan's Woes, But Not For Long \b0\par\par\uc2  By John Parry \par \uc2 862 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Juni 2002\par 21:25\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- The yen, which is flirting with six-month highs against the dollar, appears to defy gravity when  current economic data are taken into account. \par \par \uc2  But weaker economic reports may well bring the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  back to fundamentals with a bump by year end, some U.S.-based \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists say. \par \par \uc2  For now, like a cartoon character that can run in mid-air off the edge of a cliff, the yen's rally against the  dollar still appears to have some momentum left, in spite of the immediate threat of more bouts of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the  \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  via the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  aimed at slowing the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's gains. \par \par \uc2  During the past two weeks, the MOF has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on four main occasions, buying between $15 and $20 billion-worth of dollars according to rough estimates from traders. Despite its efforts, the MOF may be resigned to some modest, appreciation of the yen against the greenback in the months ahead, some U.S. based traders expect. \par \par \uc2  Larry Kantor, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at JP Morgan in New York, expects the dollar to slip to Y120.00 at  year-end, from its Y124.34 level Thursday early afternoon in New York. \par \par \uc2  Taking a longer-term view, however, Japan's fragile, nascent economic recovery may well turn the yen lower again,  Kantor and others suggest. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Japan's economy is still suffering from deflation and may not be able\par to kick start growth, Kantor argues. Over\par time, he says, if the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  were   "left to its  own\par devices," on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets, "we would see a weaker  yen."\par    Waiting For The Yen To Drop\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Other Japan-watchers expect the yen's fall from grace to arrive much\par sooner.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The earliest opportunity would be if Japan's first quarter economic\par growth data, for release early in Friday's global session, were to come\par in weaker than expectations, says Carl Weinberg, chief economist at High\par Frequency Economics in Valhalla, N.Y.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par After some disappointing data for that period, released earlier this\par week, economists are paring back their gross domestic product forecasts\par for Japan.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Capital investment by Japanese corporations plummeted 16.8% from\par year-ago levels during the January-March quarter,  according to a\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  survey. By contrast, recent U.S. data appears to\par confound the dollar's lackluster  performance against its Japanese\par rival. First quarter non-farm productivity was revised to 8.4% Friday,\par while a report Wednesday showed the U.S. service sector grew at its\par strongest rate in nearly two years in May.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Another trigger for a yen retreat could be Japan's fiscal half-year\par end at the end of September, Weinberg said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Because the dollar was around Y133.00 to the yen at the start of\par Japan's fiscal year on April 1, if the yen is stronger than that level\par on Sep. 30, then Japanese companies  "will be obliged to report \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign\par exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  losses," on their hefty portfolios of foreign assets, Weinberg\par says.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par That is a scenario Japanese policy-makers and government  officials\par will want to avoid, Weinberg says.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par If necessary, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  might step up its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets in order to weaken the yen back to around Y133.00 to\par the dollar by then, he suggests.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Indeed, the latest phase of MOF \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  designed to halt the\par yen's appreciation reflects that Japanese policy-makers are already "not\par that optimistic on the sustainability of the recovery," in Japan and are\par desperate  to defend the competitiveness of Japanese exports, says\par Paresh Upadhyaya, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  analyst at Putnam Investments in Boston.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By the end of the year, Upadhyaya expects some clear signs that the\par Japanese economy, far from rebounding, is weakening once again, which\par will likely send the dollar rising to at least Y130.00, he says.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Meanwhile, the short-term robustness of the yen on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par markets "is all a game of perception," he  added. Traders are seizing on\par what could prove to be a fleeting mirage, that Japan's dismal prospects\par seem to be a little less dire, while by contrast the dollar remains\par mired  in a spiral of negative sentiment.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Despite the more optimistic shorter term outlook for the  Japanese\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  traders are willing  to buy into,\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and sovereign debt analysts continue to warn that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par markets cannot shrug off  key structural weaknesses in Japan\par indefinitely.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The country's debt is approaching 140% of gross domestic  product,\par while its banking system, burdened with some Y150 trillion of bad and\par potentially non-performing loans, remains in disarray.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Even if Japanese GDP is shown to have grown in the first  quarter,\par Weinberg is skeptical that growth will be sustained in the second,\par "because there is continued contraction of bank credit". Without bank\par lending flowing more smoothly, Japan's economy and its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  lack\par firm foundations, he argues.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The market's conviction about the yen is skating on very thin ice,"\par Weinberg added.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By John Parry; Dow Jones Newswires\par    john.parry@dowjones.com; 201-938-2096\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1074 4083 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020020606dy6600b42\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
