\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Makes Moderate Gains On Stronger U.S. Economic Data \b0\par\par\uc2  By John Parry \par \uc2 664 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Juni 2002\par 00:53\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -- The dollar gained some ground against its main rivals Wednesday, bolstered by stronger U.S. economic data that contrasted with softer economic reports in Japan and the euro zone. \par \par \uc2  "You don't want to overdramatize the dollar coming back," said Lara Rhame, senior economist at Brown Brothers Harriman in New York, who doesn't expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to charge sharply higher amid the bearish sentiment still surrounding the greenback. \par \par \uc2  Nevertheless, Wednesday's limited gains may show that U.S. data is "still relatively strong" by comparison with other major economies, Ms. Rhame said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar jumped higher immediately after the release of an Institute for Supply Management survey for May with a reading of 60.1, revealing that the U.S. service sector grew at its fastest clip in nearly two years, driven by a strong rise in new orders. \par \par \uc2  But the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pared back some of these initial gains, showing the dollar's relative "failure to respond" to such positive data, said Ashraf Laidi, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with MG Financial Group in New York. \par \par \uc2  That reaction "reflects the stark change in sentiment against the dollar in favor of the euro," said Mr. Laidi. Instead of being hurt by comparatively disappointing European data Wednesday, the euro held its ground. \par \par \uc2  The overall euro-zone services purchasing managers' index fell to 52.1 from April's 53.3 reading. In Europe's biggest economy, Germany's service-sector activity slumped last month. The index fell to 49.1 from 52.0 in April, clearly weaker than economists' expectations. \par \par \uc2  Late Wednesday in New York, the dollar was at 124.42 yen, well off its session highs but up from 124.00 yen late Tuesday in New York. The euro was at 93.96 U.S. cents, only a touch lower than its 94.00 U.S. cents level late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was at 1.5648 Swiss francs, up from 1.5645 francs. Sterling was at $1.4590, down from $1.4592. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, data from Japan released Wednesday appeared to offer an even less optimistic outlook for that economy, driving the dollar's moderate rebound against the yen. \par \par \uc2  Capital investment by Japanese corporations plummeted 16.8% from year-ago levels during the January-March quarter, according to a \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  survey. \par \par \uc2  That indicated more weakness in Japan's economy than previously thought, casting doubts on the sustainability of the country's incipient, fragile economic rebound. \par \par \uc2  This enabled the dollar to build on some gains against the yen attained Tuesday, after the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- acting via the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to sell dollars for yen at levels between around 123.30 yen to 123.40 yen. \par \par \uc2  The greenback has also enjoyed something of a reprieve against some of the commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that have soared to multi-month highs against the U.S. dollar during recent weeks. \par \par \uc2  "There is some correction back from some of these moves," noted Ms. Rhame of Brown Brothers. The Canadian and Australian dollars "have got a little ahead of themselves, and the reaction after central banks" rate hikes shows that," Ms. Rhame added. \par \par \uc2  Both Canada and Australia have hiked rates, in the last two sessions. \par \par \uc2  Most recently, the Reserve Bank of Australia hiked interest rates by a quarter-percentage point, but because the decision was expected, the Australian dollar softened somewhat after the announcement. \par \par \uc2  After hitting a 20-month high of 57.62 U.S. cents against the U.S. dollar ahead of the RBA's rate announcement earlier Wednesday, the Australian dollar fell back in the wake of the rate hike, down to 57.34 U.S. cents late Wednesday in New York. \par \par \uc2  -- Jamie McGeever in London contributed to this report. \par \par \uc2  Write to John Parry at john.parry@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 2002 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020605dy65008y9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
