\par\par \b \uc2  NY precious metals beat retreat on profit-taking. \b0\par\par\uc2 673 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Juni 2002\par 21:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, June 5 (Reuters) - Profit-taking punched the wind out of gold's sails in New York futures trade Wednesday, with silver taking an even steeper fall as a firmer U.S. dollar eroded one incentive to extend the 2002 precious metals rally. \par \par \uc2  London traders returned from a four-day recess to find bullion traded above $330 an ounce for the first time since October 1999 while they were out. \par \par \uc2  "It was overdue. Then all the stop losses went off," said George Gero, a veteran of the precious metals futures trade and director of International Precious Metals Institute. "All the things that put it up yesterday, the same things put it down today except in reverse - the dollar and a little bit of an easing in tensions in India-Pakistan." \par \par \uc2  August gold fell $6.70 to $322.10 an ounce, trading from $328.50 to $321.10. That was down more than $10 from Tuesday's contract high at $331.50. But the metal still gleamed almost 15 percent higher than where it started the year. \par \par \uc2  The selling started at the New York close Tuesday and continued in the after-market, where the roughly 5,400 lots traded was unusually heavy business for the ACCESS electronic trading system. \par \par \uc2  Estimated total volume was a frantic 65,000 contracts. \par \par \uc2  "I heard it was an equity fund that took a punt in gold, made a few bucks and took their profit," a dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Spot gold was last at $321.15/65, down from $327.40/7.90 at the previous close. On Monday the interbank price touched $331.40, the highest since $338 on Oct 5, 1999, after the September 1999 pact to limit \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales. \par \par \uc2  London's afternoon fix Wednesday was $322 an ounce. \par \par \uc2  A recovery in the embattled U.S. stock market deprived gold of some investor flow. The Dow Jones industrials were up 59 points in Wednesday afternoon trade. \par \par \uc2  The greenback was also up from Tuesday's 16-month low against the euro and 2-1/2 year low against the Swiss franc. The dollar rebound was facilitated by Tuesday's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying dollars to stop the rally in the yen. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's weakness this year was a major catalyst for gold, because foreign investors found their bullion buying power enhanced by the strength of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The positive news on that side certainly contributed to gold going a little lower," said James Pogoda, a vice president of precious metals at Mitsubishi International Corp. "But $320 held on spot and maybe this is a place the market can try to build a base from." \par \par \uc2  Global jitters were another motivator for gold bulls. But knuckle biting about the possibility of the confrontation over Kashmir reaching critical mass appeared on the wane. \par \par \uc2  Indian Prime Minister Atal Behari Vajpayee said on Wednesday there was room for "many proposals" to help end a military standoff that has brought India to the brink of war with nuclear rival Pakistan. \par \par \uc2  The two countries have massed more than a million troops on their border and exchange constant mortar and artillery fire. India blames Pakistan for infiltration and a series of murderous attacks by Islamic militants. \par \par \uc2  July silver fell back below $5 an ounce. It closed down 18 cents at $4.945, trading $5.12 to $4.92. It broke above the psychological $5 level on Friday and reached $5.15 on Tuesday, its strongest since October 2000. \par \par \uc2  "Gold goes up, silver goes up. Gold goes down, silver goes down," said a dealer. "Silver does not have a mind of its own." \par \par \uc2  Turnover was a very busy 27,000 contracts. \par \par \uc2  Spot silver closed at $4.93/95, down from $5.10/12 late Tuesday, when it touched a 26-month high at $5.13. \par \par \uc2  NYMEX July platinum fell $3.20 to $549.80 an ounce. Spot platinum was quoted $544/548. September palladium fell $4.05 to $349.50 an ounce. Spot palladium fetched $345/352. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020605dy65010qi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
