\par\par \b \uc2  NY precious metals hit by early profit-taking. \b0\par\par\uc2 550 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Juni 2002\par 16:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, June 5 (Reuters) - COMEX gold corrected lower early Wednesday, dragging silver futures down with it, beset by heavy profit-taking off Tuesday's cycle highs and a rebounding U.S. dollar which removed an incentive to buy precious metals. \par \par \uc2  London traders returned from a four-day recess to find bullion traded above $330 an ounce for the first time since October 1999 while they were out. \par \par \uc2  At 0944 EDT, August gold was off $5.40 at $323.40 an ounce, trading $328.50 to $322.80. That was down about $8 from Tuesday's contract high at $331.50. But the precious metal is still up about 15 percent for the year. \par \par \uc2  "Gold is off because there was huge profit-taking by a fund last night on ACCESS," said a bullion dealer, referring to the COMEX electronic trading system. \par \par \uc2  The selling started shortly before the New York close Tuesday and continued in the after-market, where the roughly 5,400 lots traded was unusually heavy business for ACCESS. \par \par \uc2  "I heard it was an equity fund that took a punt in gold, made a few bucks and took their profit," the dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Spot gold quoted at $322.00/50, down from $327.40/7.90 late Tuesday when the interbank price touched $331.40, the highest since the Oct 5, 1999, peak at $338 after gold's spike on the back of the September 1999 pact to cap \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales. \par \par \uc2  London's afternoon fix Wednesday was $322.00 an ounce. \par \par \uc2  Investors tiptoed back into U.S. stocks, depriving gold of some investor flow. The Dow Jones industrials were up 29 points early Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The greenback was also up from Tuesday's 16-month low against the euro and 2-1/2 year low against the Swiss franc. The dollar rebound was facilitated by Tuesday's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying dollars to stop the rally in the yen. \par \par \uc2  The greenback's weakness this year was a major catalyst for gold because foreign investors found their bullion buying power enhanced by the strength of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Once \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  started \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the dollar, gold got sold off a little bit and equities coming back didn't help," said a floor broker. "The market needed a little bit of a healthy pullback. The small pullback is not really detrimental to the market at this point." \par \par \uc2  July silver fell below $5 an ounce for the first time since it broke above that psychological level on Friday. It was last down 14.5 cents at $4.98 an ounce, trading $5.12 to $4.965. On Tuesday it touched $5.15, its strongest since October 2000. \par \par \uc2  Dealers saw stop-loss selling at $4.98, and targeted $4.92 to $4.90. \par \par \uc2  "Gold goes up, silver goes up. Gold goes down, silver goes down," said the dealer. "Silver does not have a mind of its own." \par \par \uc2  Spot silver was quoted at $4.96/98, down from its close at $5.10/12 and Tuesday's 26-month high at $5.13. \par \par \uc2  NYMEX July platinum was $5.50 lower at $547.50 an ounce. Spot platinum was quoted $549/554. \par \par \uc2  September palladium was off 55 cents at $353 an ounce. Spot palladium fetched $345/352. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020605dy6500t0w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
