\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar holds gains, looks to US stocks for support. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Charlotte Cooper \par \uc2 624 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Juni 2002\par 14:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 5 (Reuters) - The dollar regained ground against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday, helped by signs of a mildly positive start on Wall Street and market reluctance to challenge Japanese resolve to subdue the yen. \par \par \uc2  Japanese authorities waded into the market for the fourth day in two weeks on Tuesday, selling yen for dollars in a bid to curb the yen's recent export-damaging rise. \par \par \uc2  Higher U.S. stock index futures also acted to restrain dollar bears, indicating a recent rout on Wall Street may be abating. The dollar's fortunes in recent weeks have been closely tied to those of U.S. stock markets. \par \par \uc2  "Markets are wary of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and we're waiting for U.S. equities to open up to see if they can hold to the gains indicated by the futures," said Steven Pearson, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Halifax. \par \par \uc2  By 1203 GMT the dollar was almost a quarter of a percent higher on the day against the euro at $0.9371, almost a cent up from Tuesday's 16-month low, and a quarter percent up on the Swiss franc. \par \par \uc2  It had also recovered the same degree on the yen to 124.62 yen before trimming some of the gain. A reminder warning from Japan's top financial diplomat Haruhiko Kuroda that he was still watching \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements underpinned the rise. \par \par \uc2  The next focus was the U.S. non-manufacturing Institute for Supply Management (ISM) survey for May due at 1400 GMT. \par \par \uc2  RATES IN FOCUS \par \par \uc2  Earlier, data showed the euro zone's recovery faltered in May as business in the dominant services sector contracted in Germany and grew more slowly in France and Ireland. \par \par \uc2  The survey strengthened expectations that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will keep its benchmark interest rate unchanged at 3.25 percent when it meets on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "Weaker numbers out of Europe this morning weighed a bit on the euro. Germany's service sector contracted again so if we do get a stronger ISM number that might provide the dollar with some support," Pearson said. \par \par \uc2  Comments from U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan on Tuesday indicated that the world's most powerful \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also in no hurry to raise rates. \par \par \uc2  Speaking at a panel discussion of top central bankers in Montreal, Greenspan said the outlook for the nation's economy had not changed since January and inflation remained "remarkably quiescent". \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  WATCH \par \par \uc2  While U.S. stocks were the main focus, wariness of further Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was keeping traders on their toes. \par \par \uc2  "There is still a good chance of more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and this is also keeping the dollar off its lows," said Nick Parsons, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Commerzbank. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said unless U.S. assets started to perform better, investors would continue to look for investment opportunities elsewhere. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Finance Ministry data showed last week that foreign investors bought a net 435.9 billion yen ($3.51 billion) of Japanese stocks in the week of May 20-24, up from the previous week's 359.2 billion yen. \par \par \uc2  Foreign investors have been net buyers of Japanese stocks since March. \par \par \uc2  European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data last week showed portfolio and direct investment in the euro zone saw a net inflow of 2.2 billion euros in March, compared with a net outflow of 19.8 billion euros in February. \par \par \uc2  Japanese data released earlier showed Japanese companies scaled back capital investment in January-March for a second straight quarter. \par \par \uc2  Still, most analysts expect the country's gross domestic product for that period, due on Friday, to show a strong rebound thanks to a recovery in exports and firm personal spending. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020605dy6500nxi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
