\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar off lows on hopes of end to Wall St rout. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Christina Fincher \par \uc2 573 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Juni 2002\par 12:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 5 (Reuters) - The dollar clawed back from recent lows against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday, bolstered by wariness of more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and expectations of a firmer open on Wall Street. \par \par \uc2  Japanese authorities waded into the market for the fourth day in two weeks on Tuesday, selling yen for dollars in a bid to curb the yen's recent export-damaging rise. \par \par \uc2  Firmer U.S. stock index futures also acted to restrain dollar bears, indicating a recent rout on Wall Street may be abating. The dollar's fortunes in recent weeks have been closely tied to those of U.S. stock markets. \par \par \uc2  "It looks like the freefall in U.S. stocks is stopping and this is offering the dollar some support," said Nick Parsons, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Commerzbank. \par \par \uc2  "There is still a good chance of more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and this is also keeping the dollar off its lows." \par \par \uc2  The dollar tumbled to 16-month lows against the euro and a two-and-a-half year lows against the Swiss franc on Tuesday as another poor session on Wall Street heightened concern about a decline in fund flows into dollar assets. \par \par \uc2  The dollar pushed to session highs of 124.60 yen and $0.9360 per euro in the European midsession, up a third of a percent on the day. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  WATCH \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's top financial diplomat, Haruhiko Kuroda, kept traders on their toes, reiterating that the Finance Ministry was continuing to keep a close watch on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves. \par \par \uc2  "Fear of more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is helping stabilise the dollar for now but is only likely to slow the pace of the dollar's fall," said Francesca Fornasari, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Lehman Brothers. \par \par \uc2  "Unless U.S. assets start to perform better, people will continue to look for investment opportunities elsewhere," she added. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Finance Ministry data showed last week that foreign investors bought a net 435.9 billion yen ($3.51 billion) of Japanese stocks in the week of May 20-24, up from the previous week's 359.2 billion yen. \par \par \uc2  Foreign investors have been net buyers of Japanese stocks since March. \par \par \uc2  European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data last week showed portfolio and direct investment in the euro zone saw a net inflow of 2.2 billion euros in March, compared with a net outflow of 19.8 billion euros in February. \par \par \uc2  RATES IN FOCUS \par \par \uc2  Earlier, data showed the euro zone's recovery faltered in May as business in the dominant services sector contracted in Germany and grew more slowly in France and Ireland. \par \par \uc2  The survey strengthened expectations that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will keep its benchmark interest rate unchanged at 3.25 percent when it meets in Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Comments from U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan on Tuesday indicated that the world's most powerful \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also in no hurry to raise rates. \par \par \uc2  Speaking at a panel discussion of top central bankers in Montreal, Greenspan said the outlook for the nation's economy had not changed since January and inflation remained "remarkably quiescent". \par \par \uc2  In Asian trading, data showed Japanese companies scaled back capital investment in January-March for a second straight quarter. \par \par \uc2  Still, most analysts expect the country's gross domestic product for that period, due on Friday, to show a strong rebound thanks to a recovery in exports and firm personal spending. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020605dy6500kus\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
