\par\par \b \uc2  Tokyo stocks firm, capped as capex data disappoints. \b0\par\par\uc2 475 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Juni 2002\par 03:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 5 (Reuters) - Tokyo shares firmed by mid-morning on Wednesday, led by rebounds in chipmakers such as Tokyo Electron Ltd, but the market was capped after capital spending figures came in slightly below expectations. \par \par \uc2  The benchmark Nikkei average rose 0.12 percent or 14.25 points to 11,667.32 as of 0045 GMT, while the broader TOPIX index was up 0.06 percent or 0.69 points at 1,118.63. \par \par \uc2  Standing out from the pack was Chugai Pharmaceutical Co, which soared 8.17 percent to 1,535 yen after Roche Holding AG raised its offer for shares in the drugmaker by 31 percent on Tuesday in a takeover move. \par \par \uc2  The stock was so far the second most-actively traded issue by value on the Tokyo bourse's main board. \par \par \uc2  Wednesday's data by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  showed that Japanese firms scaled back capital investment in January-March by 16.8 percent year-on-year, the second straight quarter of declines as they focused on trimming inventories. \par \par \uc2  "Today's capex figures were a little disappointing, and show that domestic demand is still very weak as Japanese firms restructure," said Tetsuya Ishijima, senior investment strategist at Okasan Securities. \par \par \uc2  "Investors are waiting for the big news day on Friday when gross domestic product figures are announced and Intel provides a trading update," he added. \par \par \uc2  The world's number-one chipmaker and technology bellwether Intel Corp is set to announce a mid-quarter earnings update on Thursday (Friday Japan time) and markets are looking for reassurance on the outlook for the tech sector in general, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  A slightly weaker yen provided relief to investors worried about its effect on exporters' overseas profits when repatriated into the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders said. \par \par \uc2  Sony Corp, the world's biggest consumer electronics maker which depends on the United States for one third of its sales, gained 1.45 percent to 6,980 yen. \par \par \uc2  In mid-morning Asian trade, the dollar was moving at around 124.09 yen slightly weaker after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the fourth time in two weeks in Tuesday U.S. trading hours, selling yen for dollars to keep the yen in check. \par \par \uc2  Chipmakers were also back in favour after the Philadelphia semiconductor index rebounded 3.44 percent on Tuesday as investors took advantage of recent declines to snap up bargains. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo Electron, Japan's biggest manufacturer of chip equipment, was up 1.81 percent at 8,450 yen, while Toshiba Corp, Japan's largest chipmaker, put on 1.53 percent to 530 yen. \par \par \uc2  Losers included casual-wear leader Fast Retailing Co Ltd, down 7.55 percent at 2,940 after it said after the market close on Tuesday its same-store sales fell 44.2 percent in May from a year earlier. \par \par \uc2  . (C) Reuters Limited 2002. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020605dy6500drx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
