\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Gets Brief Kick From BOJ  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2  By John Parry and Grainne McCarthy \par \uc2 801 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Juni 2002\par 00:56\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -- The dollar spiked higher Tuesday after the Bank of Japan's fourth recent bout of yen selling aimed at weakening the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but soon partially pared its initial gains amid a persistently despondent backdrop for dollar-denominated assets. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  took place early in Tuesday's New York session, fleetingly pushing the dollar over a yen higher. \par \par \uc2  Estimates based on the latest round of dollar purchases bring to over $20 billion the amount that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- acting via the Bank of Japan -- has forked out over the past couple of weeks. That's having a substantial impact on U.S. Treasurys, as the BOJ uses the extra dollars acquired to buy U.S. securities. \par \par \uc2  Some of the buying interest in Treasury bills in New York may be attributable to the MOF, said Gerald Lucas, senior government strategist at Merrill Lynch in New York. Although \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trades take two days to settle -- which means any MOF money at play at the short end of the Treasury market might stem from last week's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- "that doesn't mean they can't buy it forward," so the effects could be more immediate, Mr. Lucas said. \par \par \uc2  Traders reckon the BOJ spent anywhere between $1 billion and $5 billion Tuesday, taking advantage of thin \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market conditions, with London closed for the Queen's Jubilee holiday and stepping into the market after the close of trade in Tokyo. Part of the reason the BOJ and the finance ministry aren't reaping obvious success, analysts think, is due to the very fact that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is acting alone. In not going through the usual channels at the ECB or the U.S. Federal Reserve, the BOJ is arguably getting less bang for its buck, with many traders quick to dismiss the longer term effectiveness of Japan's independent efforts to run against a broad tide of bearish dollar sentiment. \par \par \uc2  Indeed, in contrast to some of the previous rounds of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- where the BOJ was apparently acting to temper what appeared to be independent yen strength -- this time its dollar purchases came at a time when the greenback is broadly weak. "We are seeing dollar weakness across the board and the BOJ is endeavoring to swim against it," said Rebecca Patterson, global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with JP Morgan in NY. \par \par \uc2  Japanese policy-makers also aimed to "temper all the good [economic] news out of Japan," by making remarks in order to "try to slow the yen's gains," noted Tim Mazanec, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Investors Bank & Trust in Boston. \par \par \uc2  Bank of Japan deputy governor Yutaka Yamaguchi said late Tuesday in New York that domestic demand is fairly weak and may not improve for some time, while economic activity is gradually stabilizing. \par \par \uc2  Among the dollar's other major rivals, the euro was initially hurt along with the yen when the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a trend that continued later in the session. \par \par \uc2  One reason why the dollar held its own against the euro was that U.S. stock markets delivered a mixed performance Tuesday, stabilizing somewhat after their plunge on Monday, while the euro may have lacked support because U.K. markets were closed again Tuesday, some strategists said. \par \par \uc2  Although most other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were overshadowed by the market's focus on the dollar versus the yen, there was some interest in the Bank of Canada's decision to raise interest rates by a quarter-percentage point, in line with expectations. \par \par \uc2  The move came in the wake of the surprise ouster of Canada's respected finance minister Paul Martin over the weekend. \par \par \uc2  After an initial sell-off the Canadian dollar "has weathered the Finance Minister's resignation very well", said Paresh Upadhyaya, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Putnam Investments in Boston. \par \par \uc2  Like other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the non-U.S. dollar bloc, including the New Zealand and Australian dollars, the Canadian unit is performing strongly against the U.S. dollar because of robust economic growth prospects and the likelihood of further monetary tightening in these countries. \par \par \uc2  Late Tuesday in New York, the dollar was at 123.99 yen, well off its post-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  highs around 124.30 yen but up from 123.56 yen late Monday in New York. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at $0.9395 down from $0.9411 late Monday. \par \par \uc2  Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was at 1.5642 Swiss francs up from 1.5616 francs late Monday in New York. Sterling was at $1.4591, down from $1.4626. \par \par \uc2  Write to John Parry at john.parry@dowjones.com and Grainne McCarthy at grainne.mccarthy@wsj.com \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 2002 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020604dy64008hl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760